window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2024-12-2", "source": "2024年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose the university newspaper is inviting submissions from the students for its coming edition on how to enrich students' knowledge of traditional Chinese culture. You are now to write an essay for submission. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "Traditional Chinese culture is a treasure that shapes our national identity, yet many students today know little\nabout it. As a contributor to our university newspaper, I would like to share several practical ways to enrich\nstudents' understanding of this precious heritage.\nTo begin with, our university could offer optional courses and regular lectures on classical literature,\ncalligraphy and traditional festivals, so as to build a solid foundation of knowledge. In addition, hands-on\nactivities are equally important. By organizing cultural experiences such as paper-cutting workshops or tea\nceremonies, students can feel the charm of tradition rather than merely reading about it. Moreover, we can\nmake full use of new media, posting short videos and articles online to spread cultural knowledge in a way that\nappeals to young people.\nIn conclusion, combining formal learning, personal experience and modern technology would help students\nappreciate traditional Chinese culture far more deeply. I sincerely hope these suggestions will benefit us all."}, "translation": {"prompt": "近年来，中国新能源汽车产业发展迅速。目前，中国新能源汽车年产量已高达近千万辆，占全球市场份额超过 60%，出口量不断创出新高。中国政府通过加大资金投入和政策引导，鼓励新能源汽车企业进行技术创新，不断提高产品在市场上的竞争力。中国新能源汽车产业的发展不仅有力推动了国内经济发展，也为全球新能源利用和环境保护做出了积极贡献。", "sample": "In recent years, China's new energy vehicle industry has developed rapidly. At present, China's annual output of new energy vehicles has reached nearly 10 million units, accounting for over 60% of the global market share, while its exports keep hitting new highs. To achieve this, the Chinese government, by increasing funding and providing policy guidance, encourages new energy vehicle enterprises to make technological innovations and continuously enhance the market competitiveness of their products. As a result, the development of this industry has not only powerfully boosted domestic economic growth, but also made positive contributions to the global use of new energy and environmental protection."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2024-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Scientists have known that depriving adult mice of vision can increase the sensitivity of individual neurons(神经元) in the part of the brain devoted to hearing. New research from biologists at the University of Maryland  __26__  that sight deprivation also changes the way brain cells  __27__  with one another, shifting the mice's sensitivity to different frequencies.\n\"This study  __28__  what we are learning about how manipulating vision can have a  __29__  effect on the ability of an animal to hear long after the window for auditory(听觉的) learning was thought to have  __30__  ,\" said Patrick Kanold, senior author of the study.\nIt was once thought that the sensory regions of the brain were not  __31__  after a critical period in childhood. This is why children learn languages much more  __32__  than adults. Kanold's earlier research disproved this idea by showing that depriving adult mice of vision for a short period increased the sensitivity of individual neurons in the auditory cortex(皮质), which is devoted to hearing.\nYoung brains wire themselves according to the sounds they hear frequently, assigning areas of the auditory cortex to  __33__  frequencies based on what they are used to hearing. The researchers found that, in adult mice, a week in the dark also changed the  __34__  of space to different frequencies.\n\"We don't know why we are seeing these patterns,\" Kanold said. \"We  __35__  that it may have to do with what the mice are paying attention to while they are in the dark.\"", "matchIntro": "If we care about plastic waste, why won't we stop drinking bottled water?\nWe have all seen the damage plastic waste is doing around the world, but sales of bottled water have continued to grow.", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "For all the innovation and choice that define the food and drink industries, if you want to make money, you could do a lot worse than fill a bottle with water and sell it. A litre of tap water, the stuff we have cleverly piped into our homes, costs less than half a penny. A litre of bottled water can cost well over a pound, especially for something fancy that has been sucked through a mountain."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Yet the bottled water market is livelier than ever. It defies our increasing awareness of the harm plastics do to the environment and a broader, growing sense that something has to change. Sales in the UK were worth a record £558.4 million this past year, an increase of 7%, according to the latest figures from the market analyst Kantar. Separate data from other analysts show that last year the British consumed more than 2.2 billion litres of bottled water, including \"take-home\" and \"on-the-go\" products. That's an annual rise in volume of 8.5%."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Environmental campaigners are struggling to understand why nations blessed with clean tap water grow only fonder of the bottle. \"It's very surprising to me,\" says Sam Chetan-Walsh, a political adviser at Greenpeace and campaigner against ocean plastic. \"Public awareness has never been higher, but the message is not quite reaching all the people it needs to.\" Where it is heard, the message is causing concern. Plastic water bottles require oceans of fossil fuels to make and ship. Additionally, single-use plastics of all types are polluting our cities and seas. Numerous documentaries have shown how plastic is ultimately killing wildlife."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Moves against various plastics have gathered pace, from shopping bags to straws and plastic-lined coffee cups. Chetan-Walsh argues that bottled water is different because the alternatives are so obvious. \"If a product that is so nakedly unnecessary can exist, then the whole system is failing,\" he says."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Hope is not entirely out of reach. The rate of growth has begun to ease (sales were up 7% in the year, compared with 8% the previous year)."}, {"label": "F", "text": "But even if large numbers of people are quitting bottled water because of care for the environment, others are taking it up. The introduction of the \"sugar tax\" on juices and soda drinks has pushed more people to bottled water, while health awareness has boosted its desirability. Tap water consumption is growing at roughly the same pace (UK consumers still drink almost three times as much tap water as bottled water)."}, {"label": "G", "text": "So the plastic tide only creeps higher. The industry is quick to point out that all its bottles are recyclable. \"But collection rates are, at the most generous estimates, 56%, so the actual recycling rate will be lower than that,\" Chetan-Walsh says. And while bottles may be recyclable, very few are made of recycled plastic. One water bottle company launched recycled half-litre \"eco\" bottles alongside its standard bottles. Another has pledged to use only recycled plastic across its range by 2025. Chetan-Walsh believes in a ban on single-use bottles. Bans do exist in some places. Organisers of a famous music festival announced that water bottles will not be sold this summer. San Francisco has banned them from city property and events. Last year, the UK government set out plans to ban single-use plastic from its estate."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Water bottlers, unsurprisingly, don't support bans. But they raise concerns about health rather than profit margins. Last month one chief executive of a water bottling company said that bans would \"result in greater consumption of sugary drinks, adding to all the health dangers of obesity, diabetes and tooth decay\". Kinvara Carey, general manager of an association of the biggest bottled water manufacturers, cites a survey in which people were asked what they would do if bottled water were not available. \"Forty-four per cent would buy another drink, which is not great, 14% would go without and 4.5% said they would find a fountain,\" she says. \"The choice is important.\""}, {"label": "I", "text": "What if fountains were more numerous, and tap water more clearly available in cafes, restaurants and elsewhere? Dozens of fountains are being installed in London. There are similar initiatives elsewhere. Before plastic and the marketing that made people think they needed bottled water in the first place, fountains were an urban fixture. Greenpeace, among others, is also pushing for a \"deposit return\" scheme. This would mean tax on bottled water would be refunded to customers who returned the plastic for recycling."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Even if bottled water sales are growing slightly more slowly, the industry is racing to adapt to changing concerns and tastes. Flavoured water is booming: sales of the sparkling variety shot up by 20%, according to the latest analyst data. Meanwhile, international water brands, as well as a range of new companies, are selling high-end reusable bottles. And if you must fill them with tap water, why not add flavouring?"}, {"label": "K", "text": "As the owner of multiple sugary drink brands and bottled water, PepsiCo is facing challenges on health and environmental fronts. Last year, the company bought SodaStream (a drinks company that sells machines for making tap water bubbly and then consumers add flavours) for $3.2 billion. It also launched a range of fancy bottles that work with tap water and flavour packets. The bottle is reusable. The packets? Not so much, and, yes, they are made of plastic, although the company invites users to post them back for recycling."}, {"label": "L", "text": "As is so often the case, clever marketing can beat reason; awareness is rarely enough. \"There is always this kind of slip between concern, intent and changed behaviour,\" says Giles Quick, an analyst of bottled water. \"The best example is five a day, the recommendation to have at least five portions of fruit and vegetables every day. Almost everyone is aware of this, but something like 15% of us achieve it.\" Unless a far-reaching bottle ban does come into force, it will be up to consumers to not only demand change—but to act themselves."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "As a university student, I've come to realise just how little I know about money. I've come to the brutal realisation that Australia's youth are being done a great disservice by not receiving any consistent financial education.\nDiving headfirst into the crash course of starting university, I've quickly tried to get myself up to speed with the financial terms of the modern era to help manage my personal finances.\nI've read some financial education books, done some online learning, and have spoken to my nearest and dearest for their pearls of financial wisdom.\nThere are undoubtedly hundreds of great resources out there for those wishing to improve their financial literacy, but while I was researching, I still kept wishing that I didn't have to play catch-up with the money world. I felt that I was sailing the financial seas with no skills and no life jacket!\nHowever, after talking to my friends who felt the same, I quickly realised that it wasn't my ignorance but the lack of financial education in our schooling system that is leaving high schoolers seriously behind in the modern world of money.\nLet's compare a theoretical financial education subject to the standard compulsory English class. On average, English may not be the most popular subject, but it's consistently on the schedule throughout high school, with all students graduating fluent in English.\nA financial education subject should do just the same. It shouldn't be just a one-day event but a course integrated throughout the whole of high school that would allow students to gradually expand their financial literacy, and would prevent the need for a 'catch up' phase once we're out on our own after graduation.\nIn the same way that learning a language or new skills takes time, building financial skills requires practice and years to gradually accumulate bits and pieces of knowledge.\nGiving young people the opportunity to become familiar with the world of money would provide them with a great advantage to enter adulthood with confidence and security so that they are able to manage their own money and look after themselves.", "47": "As a university student, I've come to realise just how little I know about money. I've come to the brutal realisation that Australia's youth are being done a great disservice by not receiving any consistent financial education.\nDiving headfirst into the crash course of starting university, I've quickly tried to get myself up to speed with the financial terms of the modern era to help manage my personal finances.\nI've read some financial education books, done some online learning, and have spoken to my nearest and dearest for their pearls of financial wisdom.\nThere are undoubtedly hundreds of great resources out there for those wishing to improve their financial literacy, but while I was researching, I still kept wishing that I didn't have to play catch-up with the money world. I felt that I was sailing the financial seas with no skills and no life jacket!\nHowever, after talking to my friends who felt the same, I quickly realised that it wasn't my ignorance but the lack of financial education in our schooling system that is leaving high schoolers seriously behind in the modern world of money.\nLet's compare a theoretical financial education subject to the standard compulsory English class. On average, English may not be the most popular subject, but it's consistently on the schedule throughout high school, with all students graduating fluent in English.\nA financial education subject should do just the same. It shouldn't be just a one-day event but a course integrated throughout the whole of high school that would allow students to gradually expand their financial literacy, and would prevent the need for a 'catch up' phase once we're out on our own after graduation.\nIn the same way that learning a language or new skills takes time, building financial skills requires practice and years to gradually accumulate bits and pieces of knowledge.\nGiving young people the opportunity to become familiar with the world of money would provide them with a great advantage to enter adulthood with confidence and security so that they are able to manage their own money and look after themselves.", "48": "As a university student, I've come to realise just how little I know about money. I've come to the brutal realisation that Australia's youth are being done a great disservice by not receiving any consistent financial education.\nDiving headfirst into the crash course of starting university, I've quickly tried to get myself up to speed with the financial terms of the modern era to help manage my personal finances.\nI've read some financial education books, done some online learning, and have spoken to my nearest and dearest for their pearls of financial wisdom.\nThere are undoubtedly hundreds of great resources out there for those wishing to improve their financial literacy, but while I was researching, I still kept wishing that I didn't have to play catch-up with the money world. I felt that I was sailing the financial seas with no skills and no life jacket!\nHowever, after talking to my friends who felt the same, I quickly realised that it wasn't my ignorance but the lack of financial education in our schooling system that is leaving high schoolers seriously behind in the modern world of money.\nLet's compare a theoretical financial education subject to the standard compulsory English class. On average, English may not be the most popular subject, but it's consistently on the schedule throughout high school, with all students graduating fluent in English.\nA financial education subject should do just the same. It shouldn't be just a one-day event but a course integrated throughout the whole of high school that would allow students to gradually expand their financial literacy, and would prevent the need for a 'catch up' phase once we're out on our own after graduation.\nIn the same way that learning a language or new skills takes time, building financial skills requires practice and years to gradually accumulate bits and pieces of knowledge.\nGiving young people the opportunity to become familiar with the world of money would provide them with a great advantage to enter adulthood with confidence and security so that they are able to manage their own money and look after themselves.", "49": "As a university student, I've come to realise just how little I know about money. I've come to the brutal realisation that Australia's youth are being done a great disservice by not receiving any consistent financial education.\nDiving headfirst into the crash course of starting university, I've quickly tried to get myself up to speed with the financial terms of the modern era to help manage my personal finances.\nI've read some financial education books, done some online learning, and have spoken to my nearest and dearest for their pearls of financial wisdom.\nThere are undoubtedly hundreds of great resources out there for those wishing to improve their financial literacy, but while I was researching, I still kept wishing that I didn't have to play catch-up with the money world. I felt that I was sailing the financial seas with no skills and no life jacket!\nHowever, after talking to my friends who felt the same, I quickly realised that it wasn't my ignorance but the lack of financial education in our schooling system that is leaving high schoolers seriously behind in the modern world of money.\nLet's compare a theoretical financial education subject to the standard compulsory English class. On average, English may not be the most popular subject, but it's consistently on the schedule throughout high school, with all students graduating fluent in English.\nA financial education subject should do just the same. It shouldn't be just a one-day event but a course integrated throughout the whole of high school that would allow students to gradually expand their financial literacy, and would prevent the need for a 'catch up' phase once we're out on our own after graduation.\nIn the same way that learning a language or new skills takes time, building financial skills requires practice and years to gradually accumulate bits and pieces of knowledge.\nGiving young people the opportunity to become familiar with the world of money would provide them with a great advantage to enter adulthood with confidence and security so that they are able to manage their own money and look after themselves.", "50": "As a university student, I've come to realise just how little I know about money. I've come to the brutal realisation that Australia's youth are being done a great disservice by not receiving any consistent financial education.\nDiving headfirst into the crash course of starting university, I've quickly tried to get myself up to speed with the financial terms of the modern era to help manage my personal finances.\nI've read some financial education books, done some online learning, and have spoken to my nearest and dearest for their pearls of financial wisdom.\nThere are undoubtedly hundreds of great resources out there for those wishing to improve their financial literacy, but while I was researching, I still kept wishing that I didn't have to play catch-up with the money world. I felt that I was sailing the financial seas with no skills and no life jacket!\nHowever, after talking to my friends who felt the same, I quickly realised that it wasn't my ignorance but the lack of financial education in our schooling system that is leaving high schoolers seriously behind in the modern world of money.\nLet's compare a theoretical financial education subject to the standard compulsory English class. On average, English may not be the most popular subject, but it's consistently on the schedule throughout high school, with all students graduating fluent in English.\nA financial education subject should do just the same. It shouldn't be just a one-day event but a course integrated throughout the whole of high school that would allow students to gradually expand their financial literacy, and would prevent the need for a 'catch up' phase once we're out on our own after graduation.\nIn the same way that learning a language or new skills takes time, building financial skills requires practice and years to gradually accumulate bits and pieces of knowledge.\nGiving young people the opportunity to become familiar with the world of money would provide them with a great advantage to enter adulthood with confidence and security so that they are able to manage their own money and look after themselves.", "51": "Chocolates save us from many things, especially emotional distress. They comfort us in times of trouble, calming down a racing heart by channelling happy calories inside us. We all have faith in chocolates to delight us in an instant!\nRecently, chocolate lovers were heartbroken as scientists claimed that they can become extinct by 2050! But hey, we have some happy news for you. Scientists can still help save chocolates from dying out! If you are not aware as to why scientists made the statement about the death of this wonderful thing, let us tell you the facts. Chocolate trees, whose seeds are used to make chocolate, grow in the tropical plant world and require very specific weather conditions to prosper.\nNow, fifty percent of the world's cocoa(可可) beans come from two countries in West Africa: Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana. Scientists believe that both of these countries will experience a 3.8°F temperature increase by 2050 due to global warming, endangering the cacao(可可树) farms in the rainforests. These farms will then have to be shifted to cooler mountainous areas, which are the natural habitat of wildlife. This will lead to some tough decisions: whether to grow chocolate or save wildlife.\nUnfortunately, the crisis of global warming has already had a serious negative impact on cacao farms' yields, leading to the prices of chocolates skyrocketing.\nScientists, however, are trying to find a long-term and eco-friendly solution to this problem! They are trying to modify the species with a gene-editing technology, which will transform the seedlings into a species that survives even in a drier and warmer climate.\nAccording to a report by The Business Insider, in the University of California's new bio-sciences building, tiny green cacao seedlings are lined up in refrigerated greenhouses for a new experiment by using a technology called CRISPR. By manipulating the DNA of plants, this technology is already being used across the world to make plants tougher and cheaper. Similarly, in this unconventional experiment, scientists will make tiny, precise changes to the DNA of the seedlings to make the cocoa crops survive in warmer and drier climates.", "52": "Chocolates save us from many things, especially emotional distress. They comfort us in times of trouble, calming down a racing heart by channelling happy calories inside us. We all have faith in chocolates to delight us in an instant!\nRecently, chocolate lovers were heartbroken as scientists claimed that they can become extinct by 2050! But hey, we have some happy news for you. Scientists can still help save chocolates from dying out! If you are not aware as to why scientists made the statement about the death of this wonderful thing, let us tell you the facts. Chocolate trees, whose seeds are used to make chocolate, grow in the tropical plant world and require very specific weather conditions to prosper.\nNow, fifty percent of the world's cocoa(可可) beans come from two countries in West Africa: Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana. Scientists believe that both of these countries will experience a 3.8°F temperature increase by 2050 due to global warming, endangering the cacao(可可树) farms in the rainforests. These farms will then have to be shifted to cooler mountainous areas, which are the natural habitat of wildlife. This will lead to some tough decisions: whether to grow chocolate or save wildlife.\nUnfortunately, the crisis of global warming has already had a serious negative impact on cacao farms' yields, leading to the prices of chocolates skyrocketing.\nScientists, however, are trying to find a long-term and eco-friendly solution to this problem! They are trying to modify the species with a gene-editing technology, which will transform the seedlings into a species that survives even in a drier and warmer climate.\nAccording to a report by The Business Insider, in the University of California's new bio-sciences building, tiny green cacao seedlings are lined up in refrigerated greenhouses for a new experiment by using a technology called CRISPR. By manipulating the DNA of plants, this technology is already being used across the world to make plants tougher and cheaper. Similarly, in this unconventional experiment, scientists will make tiny, precise changes to the DNA of the seedlings to make the cocoa crops survive in warmer and drier climates.", "53": "Chocolates save us from many things, especially emotional distress. They comfort us in times of trouble, calming down a racing heart by channelling happy calories inside us. We all have faith in chocolates to delight us in an instant!\nRecently, chocolate lovers were heartbroken as scientists claimed that they can become extinct by 2050! But hey, we have some happy news for you. Scientists can still help save chocolates from dying out! If you are not aware as to why scientists made the statement about the death of this wonderful thing, let us tell you the facts. Chocolate trees, whose seeds are used to make chocolate, grow in the tropical plant world and require very specific weather conditions to prosper.\nNow, fifty percent of the world's cocoa(可可) beans come from two countries in West Africa: Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana. Scientists believe that both of these countries will experience a 3.8°F temperature increase by 2050 due to global warming, endangering the cacao(可可树) farms in the rainforests. These farms will then have to be shifted to cooler mountainous areas, which are the natural habitat of wildlife. This will lead to some tough decisions: whether to grow chocolate or save wildlife.\nUnfortunately, the crisis of global warming has already had a serious negative impact on cacao farms' yields, leading to the prices of chocolates skyrocketing.\nScientists, however, are trying to find a long-term and eco-friendly solution to this problem! They are trying to modify the species with a gene-editing technology, which will transform the seedlings into a species that survives even in a drier and warmer climate.\nAccording to a report by The Business Insider, in the University of California's new bio-sciences building, tiny green cacao seedlings are lined up in refrigerated greenhouses for a new experiment by using a technology called CRISPR. By manipulating the DNA of plants, this technology is already being used across the world to make plants tougher and cheaper. Similarly, in this unconventional experiment, scientists will make tiny, precise changes to the DNA of the seedlings to make the cocoa crops survive in warmer and drier climates.", "54": "Chocolates save us from many things, especially emotional distress. They comfort us in times of trouble, calming down a racing heart by channelling happy calories inside us. We all have faith in chocolates to delight us in an instant!\nRecently, chocolate lovers were heartbroken as scientists claimed that they can become extinct by 2050! But hey, we have some happy news for you. Scientists can still help save chocolates from dying out! If you are not aware as to why scientists made the statement about the death of this wonderful thing, let us tell you the facts. Chocolate trees, whose seeds are used to make chocolate, grow in the tropical plant world and require very specific weather conditions to prosper.\nNow, fifty percent of the world's cocoa(可可) beans come from two countries in West Africa: Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana. Scientists believe that both of these countries will experience a 3.8°F temperature increase by 2050 due to global warming, endangering the cacao(可可树) farms in the rainforests. These farms will then have to be shifted to cooler mountainous areas, which are the natural habitat of wildlife. This will lead to some tough decisions: whether to grow chocolate or save wildlife.\nUnfortunately, the crisis of global warming has already had a serious negative impact on cacao farms' yields, leading to the prices of chocolates skyrocketing.\nScientists, however, are trying to find a long-term and eco-friendly solution to this problem! They are trying to modify the species with a gene-editing technology, which will transform the seedlings into a species that survives even in a drier and warmer climate.\nAccording to a report by The Business Insider, in the University of California's new bio-sciences building, tiny green cacao seedlings are lined up in refrigerated greenhouses for a new experiment by using a technology called CRISPR. By manipulating the DNA of plants, this technology is already being used across the world to make plants tougher and cheaper. Similarly, in this unconventional experiment, scientists will make tiny, precise changes to the DNA of the seedlings to make the cocoa crops survive in warmer and drier climates.", "55": "Chocolates save us from many things, especially emotional distress. They comfort us in times of trouble, calming down a racing heart by channelling happy calories inside us. We all have faith in chocolates to delight us in an instant!\nRecently, chocolate lovers were heartbroken as scientists claimed that they can become extinct by 2050! But hey, we have some happy news for you. Scientists can still help save chocolates from dying out! If you are not aware as to why scientists made the statement about the death of this wonderful thing, let us tell you the facts. Chocolate trees, whose seeds are used to make chocolate, grow in the tropical plant world and require very specific weather conditions to prosper.\nNow, fifty percent of the world's cocoa(可可) beans come from two countries in West Africa: Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana. Scientists believe that both of these countries will experience a 3.8°F temperature increase by 2050 due to global warming, endangering the cacao(可可树) farms in the rainforests. These farms will then have to be shifted to cooler mountainous areas, which are the natural habitat of wildlife. This will lead to some tough decisions: whether to grow chocolate or save wildlife.\nUnfortunately, the crisis of global warming has already had a serious negative impact on cacao farms' yields, leading to the prices of chocolates skyrocketing.\nScientists, however, are trying to find a long-term and eco-friendly solution to this problem! They are trying to modify the species with a gene-editing technology, which will transform the seedlings into a species that survives even in a drier and warmer climate.\nAccording to a report by The Business Insider, in the University of California's new bio-sciences building, tiny green cacao seedlings are lined up in refrigerated greenhouses for a new experiment by using a technology called CRISPR. By manipulating the DNA of plants, this technology is already being used across the world to make plants tougher and cheaper. Similarly, in this unconventional experiment, scientists will make tiny, precise changes to the DNA of the seedlings to make the cocoa crops survive in warmer and drier climates."}, "questions": [{"id": "2024-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "Why are families across the U.S. looking to this winter with fear?", "options": ["A) They will see a 27% higher price to buy natural gas.", "B) They will have insufficient heating oil to keep warm.", "C) They will have a costly bill to pay to heat their homes.", "D) They will experience the coldest winter in four decades."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They will have a costly bill to pay to heat their homes.\n【定位】U.S. families are looking to the winter with fear as energy costs soar. The Energy Department\nprojects heating bills will jump 28% this winter for those who rely on natural gas.\n随着能源成本飙升，美国家庭对这个冬天满怀恐惧。能源部预计，对那些依赖天然气的家庭\n来说，今冬取暖账单将上涨 28%。\n【信号】as 引出发愁的直接原因。原文 looking to the winter with fear as energy costs soar 中\n的 as 直接把‘发愁’与‘能源成本飙升’扣在一起，as 后面就是答案信号——发愁的原因是费用\n涨，后文 heating bills will jump 28% 进一步坐实是‘取暖账单暴涨’。\n【替换】heating bills will jump 28% ↔ a costly bill to pay to heat their homes\n‘账单涨28%’概括为‘贵账单’\nenergy costs soar ↔ costly\nsoar 对应 costly\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文确实念了 27%，但 Heating oil is projected to be 27% higher 说的是‘取暖用油’涨\n27%；选项 a 27% higher price to buy natural gas 把 27% 安到了天然气头上（天然气其\n实是涨 28%），数字盗用且对错对象。\n借用原文：Heating oil is projected to be 27% higher\nB) 过度推断\n原文有 supplies have been reduced（供应减少）和大量 heating oil 字样，选项\ninsufficient heating oil to keep warm 借这些词造出‘油不够保暖’的说法，但原文讲的是\n‘费用贵’，从未说会‘缺油到取不了暖’。\n借用原文：supplies have been reduced\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文 four decades 出现在 consumer prices growing 6.6%. That's the fastest pace in\nfour decades，说的是‘物价涨速四十年最快’；选项 the coldest winter in four decades\n却把 four decades 挪去形容‘四十年最冷的冬天’，原文根本没谈天气冷暖。\n借用原文：That's the fastest pace in four decades", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did the federal government do last month?", "options": ["A) Provided additional funding to Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program.", "B) Assured U.S. citizens of an increase in energy supplies by the end of this year.", "C) Predicted a 10% jump in heating bills for most U.S. households this winter.", "D) Decided on this year's energy assistance package of $8 billion in total."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\nProvided additional funding to Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program.\n【定位】Last month, the federal government added $1 billion in funding to Low Income Home Energy\nAssistance Program, bringing the total to $4.8 billion.\n上个月，联邦政府向‘低收入家庭能源援助项目’追加了 10 亿美元资金，使总额达到 48 亿\n美元。\n【信号】Last month 与题干同词定位。原文 Last month, the federal government added \\$1\nbillion in funding 开头的 Last month 与题干 last month 完全对上，紧跟的动词 added\n就是答案信号——政府上月‘追加了拨款’。\n【替换】added \\$1 billion in funding to Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program ↔\nProvided additional funding to Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program\nadded funding=provided additional funding\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n原文出现过 supplies have been reduced（供应减少），选项 Assured U.S. citizens of\nan increase in energy supplies 反过来说政府‘保证能源供应会增加’，方向相反，原文也无\n任何‘保证’动作。\n借用原文：supplies have been reduced\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文 10% 出自 electricity 10% higher（电费涨 10%），且作预测的是能源部不是政府这\n一动作主体；选项 Predicted a 10% jump in heating bills 把电费的 10% 改成‘取暖账\n单’、又把‘预测’这一动作安给了政府上月的作为，数字与主体双错。\n借用原文：electricity 10% higher\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文 a reduction from last year's energy assistance package of over \\$8 billion 明说\n80 亿是‘去年’援助方案、且今年比它‘减少’了；选项 Decided on this year's energy\nassistance package of \\$8 billion in total 把去年的 80 亿说成‘今年合计敲定 80 亿’，年\n份与金额张冠李戴。\n借用原文：last year's energy assistance package of over \\$8 billion\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Many Americans think they make healthy food choices, but they may be viewing their diet too\noptimistically. That's the main finding of a new study by researchers at the U.S. Department of\nAgriculture in Stoneville, Mississippi.\n许多美国人认为自己做出了健康的食物选择，但他们可能对自己的饮食过于乐观了。这是美国农业部位于\n密西西比州Stoneville的研究人员一项新研究的主要发现。\n2 The study aimed to identify disconnects between how healthily Americans think they eat and how they\nactually do. For the study, the researchers asked participants to rate their diet as excellent, very good,\ngood, fair or poor. Folks also completed 24-hour food questionnaires. Then, the researchers compared\nanswers to see how well responses to the two exercises matched up.\n该研究旨在找出美国人认为自己吃得有多健康与实际情况之间的差距。在研究中，研究人员要求参与者将\n自己的饮食评为优秀、非常好、好、一般或差。参与者还完成了24小时食物问卷。然后，研究人员比较了\n答案，看两项调查的回答匹配程度如何。\n3 Of more than 9,700 people, roughly 85 percent were off base when asked to rate the quality of their\ndiet, and almost all overestimated how healthy it was. Those who rated their diet as poor tended to be\nfar more accurate. Their rating matched that of the researchers more than nine times out of ten.\n在9700多人中，大约85%的人在被要求评价自己的饮食质量时偏离了实际情况，而且几乎所有人都高估\n了饮食的健康程度。那些将自己的饮食评为差的人往往准确得多。他们的评价在十次中有九次以上与研究\n人员的评价相符。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What is the main finding of a new study about many Americans?", "options": ["A) They care less about their diet now than before.", "B) They may lack adequate knowledge of healthy food.", "C) They may hold too optimistic a view about their diet.", "D) They pay insufficient attention to their choice of food."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题） They may hold too optimistic a view about their diet.\n【定位】Many Americans think they make healthy food choices, but they may be viewing their diet\ntoo optimistically. That's the main finding of a new study by researchers at the U.S.\nDepartment of Agriculture in Stoneville, Mississippi.\n很多美国人以为自己在做健康的食物选择，但他们对自己饮食的看法可能太乐观了。这正是\n美国农业部研究人员一项新研究的主要发现。\n【信号】转折but+点题句That's the main finding。答案藏在转折之后：but they may be\nviewing their diet too optimistically 才是真意，紧接着一句 That's the main finding\nof a new study 直接盖章这就是\"主要发现\"，双重信号（but转折 + 点题句）锁定它是答\n案句。\n【替换】viewing their diet too optimistically ↔ hold too optimistic a view about their diet\n副词转形容词的同义改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\ncare less about their diet now than before 说\"现在比从前更不在乎饮食\"，录音通篇没\n有\"今昔对比\"，更没说他们\"不在乎\"——恰恰相反，他们\"以为\"自己吃得健康。属凭空捏造\n的对比。\nB) 偷换概念\nlack adequate knowledge of healthy food 把问题归为\"缺乏健康饮食知识\"，但录音说的\n是他们\"看得太乐观\"（认知偏差），不是\"不懂\"。盗用了 healthy food 这一原词，却把病\n因悄悄换掉。\n借用原文：Many Americans think they make healthy food choices\nD) 原词陷阱\npay insufficient attention to their choice of food 说他们\"对选食不够上心\"，但录音里他\n们其实很在意、还自认 make healthy food choices，问题是\"高估\"而非\"不上心\"。盗用\nchoices 一词来制造\"听到 food choices 就想选\"的诱惑。\n借用原文：make healthy food choices", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "Who tended to rate their diet more accurately in the survey?", "options": ["A) Those who finished 24-hour food questionnaires.", "B) Those who fully understood the rating scale.", "C) Those who cared about food quality.", "D) Those who rated their diet as poor."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Those who rated their diet as poor.\n【定位】Those who rated their diet as poor tended to be far more accurate. Their rating matched that\nof the researchers more than nine times out of ten.\n那些把自己饮食评为\"差\"的人，往往要准确得多。他们的评分有十之九以上与研究人员的评\n定相符。\n【信号】题干词more accurate原句复现做信号。题干问 more accurately，录音里 tended to be\nfar more accurate 几乎原样出现，这处与题干高度重合的句子就是答案句；紧跟的 more\nthan nine times out of ten 用数字坐实其\"更准\"。\n【替换】Those who rated their diet as poor ↔ Those who rated their diet as poor\n主语整句几乎原词搬运\ntended to be far more accurate ↔ 4D\n谓语印证人群身份\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThose who finished 24-hour food questionnaires. 搬来录音 completed 24-hour food\nquestionnaires，但那只是\"所有参与者都做的一个环节\"，录音从没说\"填了这问卷的人评得\n更准\"。专骗听到 24-hour 这个醒目数字就选的人。\n借用原文：Folks also completed 24-hour food questionnaires\nB) 未提及\nThose who fully understood the rating scale. 说\"完全看懂评分表的人\"，录音只让参与\n者用 excellent, very good, good, fair or poor 评级，从没区分\"谁更懂评分表\"，更没说这\n类人更准。属凭空添加。\nC) 偷换概念\nThose who cared about food quality. 盗用录音 rate the quality of their diet 里的\nquality 一词，编出\"在乎食物质量的人\"，但录音问的是评级准不准，与\"在不在乎质量\"无\n关，是借原词造的假分类。\n借用原文：rate the quality of their diet", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the report say dozens of Indians did last week?", "options": ["A) They experienced a heartbreaking moment.", "B) They gathered together to rescue an elephant.", "C) They heard a noise from the bottom of a well.", "D) They found an elephant wandering around a well."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They gathered together to rescue an elephant.\n【定位】It was a heartwarming moment that dozens of Indian rescuers rallied together to use a crane\nin order to save an elephant trapped at the bottom of a 20-foot well.\n那是一个暖心的时刻：数十名印度营救人员齐心协力，动用一台起重机去解救一头被困在\n20 英尺深井底部的大象。\n【信号】题干原话定位开头句。题干里的 dozens of Indians 直接对应录音首句 dozens of Indian\nrescuers rallied together，新闻第一题答案多在开头，听到这群人\"齐心协力去救大象\"就\n锁定此句。\n【替换】dozens of Indian rescuers rallied together ↔ They gathered together\nrallied=gathered 同义替换\nto save an elephant ↔ to rescue an elephant\nsave=rescue 同义替换\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音首句明明是 a heartwarming moment（暖心时刻），选项却写成 a heartbreaking\nmoment（令人心碎的时刻），盗用了高度相似的 heart- 开头词把情绪极性反了过来，听\n到 heart 就选会中招。\n借用原文：It was a heartwarming moment\nC) 张冠李戴\n\"听到井里有声音\"确实在录音里 The army officers heard a noise coming from the\nwell，但那是\"军官\"听到的，不是这群营救人员做的，选项把军官的动作安到了 dozens of\nIndians 头上。\n借用原文：The army officers heard a noise coming from the well\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说的是大象自己 wandered out of the jungle and fell into the well，是大象游荡，\n不是这群印度人\"发现一头在井边游荡的大象\"，选项盗用 wander 和 well 拼出一个原文没\n说的动作。\n借用原文：The elephant wandered out of the jungle and fell into the well", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "Why did the army officers call the local forest department?", "options": ["A) To borrow their crane.", "B) To ask them for help.", "C) To report the strange noise.", "D) To get food for the elephant."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题） To ask them for help.\n【定位】The officers then threw in some food for the creature and called the local forest department\nfor help.\n随后军官们往井里扔了些食物给这头大象，并打电话向当地林业部门求助。\n【信号】for help 点出目的。答案信号是表目的的 called the local forest department for help 里\n的 for help——\"call ... for help\"直接说明打电话就是\"为了求助\"，这是目的题的落点。\n【替换】called the local forest department for help ↔ To ask them for help\nfor help=ask for help 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\ncrane（起重机）确实在录音里出现过 use a crane，但那是后来营救时用的工具，录音从没\n说打电话是\"为了借起重机\"，选项盗用 crane 这个原词制造\"听到了就想选\"的诱惑。\n借用原文：to use a crane\nC) 答非所问\n\"井里传来奇怪声音\"在录音里是军官自己 heard a noise coming from the well，他们打电\n话是去求助、不是\"去报告这个声音\"，选项把\"听到声音\"硬说成打电话的目的。\n借用原文：The army officers heard a noise coming from the well\nD) 原词陷阱\n\"给大象弄吃的\"沾着录音里 threw in some food for the creature，但扔食物是军官自己做\n的另一件事，不是打电话的目的，选项盗用 food 这个原词来误导。\n借用原文：threw in some food for the creature", "points": ["听力·长对话", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the news report say happened finally?", "options": ["A) The elephant was able to return to the jungle.", "B) The army officers were examined for injuries.", "C) The army officers were rewarded for their rescue efforts.", "D) The elephant was given medical treatment for many days."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The elephant was able to return to the jungle.\n【定位】The elephant was kept under close supervision for a few days before it was successfully\nreleased back into the wild.\n这头大象被严密看护了几天，之后才被成功放归野外。\n【信号】尾句 released 收束结局。答案信号是录音最后一句的 successfully released back into\nthe wild——\"成功放归野外\"是整件事的收尾结局，新闻末题答案就在此。\n【替换】released back into the wild ↔ return to the jungle\nthe wild=the jungle 同义\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n\"检查伤情\"在录音里有 for evaluation of any injuries，但被检查的是大象，不是\"军官\"，\n选项把检查对象从大象偷换成 army officers，听到 injuries 就选会错。\n借用原文：taken to an examining room for evaluation of any injuries\nC) 未提及\n录音通篇没有说军官因营救\"受到奖励\"，reward 是凭空添加的情节，属于原文根本没提到\n的内容。\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说的是 kept under close supervision for a few days（看护了几天），选项却写成\ngiven medical treatment for many days，把\"几天看护\"夸成\"很多天的治疗\"，且最终结\n局是\"放归\"而非\"治疗\"，盗用 days 偷改了时长与重点。\n借用原文：kept under close supervision for a few days", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "Why has the man stopped paying online for the last three months?", "options": ["A) He found it had caused him much inconvenience.", "B) It led him to spend more money than necessary.", "C) It was much less secure than paying with cash.", "D) He wanted to be less dependent on his phone."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It led him to spend more money than necessary.\n【定位】No, they are safe. But paying online is too convenient and I spent too much money on things I\ndidn't need.\n不，线上付款是安全的。但线上付款太方便了，我在不需要的东西上花了太多钱。\n【信号】转折 But 之后才是真因。男方先用 No, they are safe. 否掉\"不安全\"的猜测，再用 But 引\n出真正理由 I spent too much money on things I didn't need——转折后那半句才是答\n案句。\n【替换】I spent too much money on things I didn't need ↔\nIt led him to spend more money than necessary.\n\"不需要的东西上多花钱\"=\"花了多于必要的钱\"\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音说线上付款 too convenient（太方便了），选项却说 caused him much\ninconvenience（带来很多不便）——A 盗了 convenient 这个原词却反着说，方向正相\n反。\n借用原文：paying online is too convenient\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 much less secure（不太安全）盗用了\"安全\"这一话题，但录音明确说 they are safe\n——他恰恰否认了安全顾虑，C 取的是被否定的方向。\n借用原文：No, they are safe\nD) 未提及\nless dependent on his phone（少依赖手机）听着合理，但录音通篇只说花钱失控，没有\n一句提到\"想摆脱对手机的依赖\"，属凭常识硬补。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What problem does the woman say she may also have?", "options": ["A) Living beyond her means.", "B) Lack of budgeting strategies.", "C) Fashion addiction.", "D) Impulse purchasing."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Impulse purchasing.\n【定位】Oh, impulse purchases can be an issue for me, too. I've definitely bought clothes and shoes I\ndidn't need because they were on sale.\n哦，冲动购物对我来说也是个问题。我确实买过自己并不需要的衣服和鞋子，就因为它们在\n打折。\n【信号】for me, too 的自我承认。女方用 can be an issue for me, too（对我来说也是个问题）直\n接认账，too 是\"我也有\"的信号，紧接着的 impulse purchases 就是她自陈的毛病。\n【替换】impulse purchases can be an issue for me, too ↔ Impulse purchasing.\npurchases=purchasing，几乎原义对应\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nLiving beyond her means（花超过收入）听着像\"花太多\"，但女方只承认冲动购物，没说\n自己入不敷出，是程度上的过度引申。\nB) 未提及\nLack of budgeting strategies（缺预算策略）原文无对应；女方反而说自己有 review my\nspending every night 的办法，谈不上缺策略。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里她提到 bought clothes and shoes，选项 Fashion addiction（迷恋时尚成瘾）借\n这两个词把\"偶尔冲动买\"夸成\"成瘾\"，听到 clothes/shoes 就选会中招。\n借用原文：bought clothes and shoes I didn't need", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the man say he could do at the canteen?", "options": ["A) Eat healthier food with less money.", "B) Order food like burgers and chips.", "C) Avoid getting addicted to junk food", "D) Pay either in cash or with his phone."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Eat healthier food with less money.\n【定位】I'd use my phone to order food, like burgers and other junk, instead of going to the canteen,\nwhere I could get a healthier meal for half the price.\n我会用手机点外卖，比如汉堡之类的垃圾食品，而不是去食堂——在食堂我能用一半的价钱\n吃到更健康的一餐。\n【信号】canteen 后的 where 从句给好处。答案就在 going to the canteen, where I could get a\nhealthier meal for half the price——canteen 后面的 where 从句直接说出在食堂能得到\n什么，是答案句的落点。\n【替换】a healthier meal for half the price ↔ Eat healthier food with less money.\nhalf the price=less money，meal=food\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nOrder food like burgers and chips 直接盗用录音的 burgers，但那是他用手机点的外\n卖、是 instead of（而不是）去食堂的反面行为，根本不是\"在食堂能做的\"。\n借用原文：order food, like burgers and other junk\nC) 过度推断\n录音有 other junk（垃圾食品），选项 Avoid getting addicted to junk food 借 junk 编\n出\"避免吃上瘾\"，但原文只说食堂更健康便宜，没提\"上瘾\"或\"避免上瘾\"。\n借用原文：burgers and other junk\nD) 原词陷阱\nPay either in cash or with his phone 把前文的\"现金/手机付款\"搬来，但题问的是\"在食堂\n能做什么\"，付款方式与此无关，是套用别处原词的干扰。\n借用原文：use my phone to order food", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What is now just a habit for the man?", "options": ["A) Making a shopping list.", "B) Sticking to his budget.", "C) Withdrawing cash only once a month.", "D) Thinking twice before buying something."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Sticking to his budget.\n【定位】After that I refused to spend more than what I had budgeted, and by now it's just a habit.\n在那之后我就拒绝花超过我预算的钱，到现在这已经成了一种习惯。\n【信号】by now it's just a habit 收尾点题。by now it's just a habit 是收束信号，紧贴它前面的\nrefused to spend more than what I had budgeted 就是\"成了习惯\"的那件事——答案在\n习惯标志词紧邻的前半句。\n【替换】refused to spend more than what I had budgeted ↔ Sticking to his budget.\n\"不超预算花钱\"=\"守住预算\"\n【排除】A) 未提及\nMaking a shopping list 是常见省钱招，但录音通篇没提\"列清单\"，是凭常识补的干扰。\nC) 与原文相反\nWithdrawing cash only once a month 借用前文取现的场景，但录音说他 go to the\nbank once a week（每周一次）而非 once a month，频次被偷改，且这不是收尾点出\n的\"习惯\"。\n借用原文：I go to the bank once a week\nD) 答非所问\nThinking twice before buying something 与\"控制冲动\"沾边，但男方收尾点出的习惯明\n确是\"不超预算\"，没有说\"买前三思\"成了习惯，方向虽近却非他的原话。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Why is the woman opposed to their son joining a sport like soccer?", "options": ["A) He does not enjoy any ball games.", "B) He has to prepare for the future.", "C) He has breathing problems.", "D) He is not up to anything challenging."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He has breathing problems.\n【定位】W: Um, I don't think he should be doing something so challenging with all his\nbreathing issues.\n女：嗯，我觉得他不该去做这么有挑战性的运动，他还有那一身呼吸方面的毛病呢。\n【信号】反对句后跟出理由。女方先表态 I don't think he should be doing something so\nchallenging（反对做高强度运动），紧接着用 with 引出真正的缘由 with all his\nbreathing issues——\"反对+because式补充\"就是答案信号，落点在 with 之后的原因。\n【替换】breathing issues ↔ breathing problems\nissues=problems 同义换词\n【排除】A) 未提及\nHe does not enjoy any ball games. 说他\"不喜欢任何球类\"，可女方通篇都在给儿子挑球\n类（羽毛球、排球），根本没说他不喜欢球，属凭空捏造。\nB) 原词陷阱\nHe has to prepare for the future. 里的\"为将来做准备\"确实在录音里出现过，但那是男方\neven if it might prepare him for the future 说的、且是他要否定的东西，不是女方反对足\n球的理由——典型的\"听到原词就想选\"。\n借用原文：even if it might prepare him for the future\nD) 偷换概念\nHe is not up to anything challenging.（应付不了有挑战的事）借用了原文 something\nso challenging 里的 challenging，听着很顺，但女方反对的根子是\"呼吸问题\"，\"高强\n度\"只是结果、不是原因——把原因偷换成了表象。\n借用原文：something so challenging", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the man suggest their son try in order to understand team spirit?", "options": ["A) Soccer.", "B) Badminton.", "C) Volleyball.", "D) Basketball."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Basketball.\n【定位】M: He could try basketball since many of the courts are indoors now and it's a sport that\ncould help him understand team spirit while cooperating with others.\n男：他可以试试篮球，现在很多球场都在室内了，而且这是一项能帮他在与人协作中理解团\n队精神的运动。\n【信号】建议词+目的吻合。男方用建议句 He could try basketball，并紧接着点出目的 could\nhelp him understand team spirit，与题干\"in order to understand team spirit\"严丝合\n缝——\"建议句 + 目的状语命中题干限定\"就是答案信号。\n【替换】He could try basketball ↔ Basketball.\n运动名原词对应\nunderstand team spirit ↔ Basketball.\n目的对上题干限定\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nSoccer. 确实被念到，但那是男方开头 like soccer 提的、并非\"为理解团队精神\"而提，不满\n足题干限定，听到 soccer 就选会落坑。\n借用原文：like soccer\nB) 张冠李戴\nBadminton. 是女方 like badminton 建议的室内运动，既不是男方提的、也与团队精神无\n关，张冠李戴又跑题。\n借用原文：like badminton\nC) 原词陷阱\nVolleyball. 是女方在第4段 what about volleyball 才提的，且理由是社交和奖学金，不是\n男方、也不是为团队精神，纯属原词诱选。\n借用原文：what about volleyball", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What is one of the woman's considerations in choosing a sport for her son?", "options": ["A) The possibilities of joining the school sports team.", "B) The opportunities of winning some championships.", "C) The chances of getting an athletic scholarship later on.", "D) The odds of becoming a professional sportsman someday."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The chances of getting an athletic scholarship later on.\n【定位】W: It's not as popular as some sports, which means he has better odds of possibly getting an\nathletic scholarship in the future.\n女：它不像有些运动那么热门，这意味着他将来更有机会拿到体育奖学金。\n【信号】which means引出考量。女方在介绍排球时用 which means he has better odds of\npossibly getting an athletic scholarship in the future 道出她看重的好处——\"which\nmeans + 具体好处\"就是她考量的落点，正是答案信号。\n【替换】getting an athletic scholarship in the future ↔ getting an athletic scholarship later on\nin the future=later on\nbetter odds of possibly getting ↔ The chances of getting\nodds=chances 同义\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThe possibilities of joining the school sports team. 对应的\"加入校队\"是男方在第3段\njoin the school basketball team 提的，且针对篮球，不是女方选排球的考量，说话人和项\n目都错。\n借用原文：join the school basketball team\nB) 张冠李戴\nThe opportunities of winning some championships. 里的\"赢冠军\"出自男方第3段\nwinning a game or even a championship，是他讲团队运动好处时说的，与女方无关。\n借用原文：winning a game or even a championship\nD) 过度推断\nThe odds of becoming a professional sportsman someday. 盗用了女方原句里的\nodds，又把\"拿奖学金\"夸大成\"当职业运动员\"，原文根本没说要走职业道路，是顺着 odds\n往前多推了一步。\n借用原文：better odds of possibly getting an athletic scholarship in the future", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the man say about beach volleyball?", "options": ["A) It differs entirely from indoor volleyball.", "B) It appeals to both schoolgirls and schoolboys.", "C) It is less popular than indoor sports in the country.", "D) It is completely different from other outdoor games."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It differs entirely from indoor volleyball.\n【定位】M: As I've said many times before, beach volleyball and indoor volleyball are\ncompletely different.\n男：我之前说过很多次了，沙滩排球和室内排球是完全不一样的。\n【信号】强调句直接表态。男方用 As I've said many times before（我说过很多遍了）加重语气，\n再直给观点 beach volleyball and indoor volleyball are completely different——这\n种\"强调铺垫+直接表态\"就是答案句，落点清清楚楚。\n【替换】beach volleyball and indoor volleyball are completely different ↔\nIt differs entirely from indoor volleyball.\ncompletely different=differs entirely\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nIt appeals to both schoolgirls and schoolboys. 对应的\"男女都参与\"是女方第4段 he\ncould socialize with both girls and boys 说普通排球时讲的，既不是男方、也不是评沙滩\n排球。\n借用原文：he could socialize with both girls and boys\nC) 张冠李戴\nIt is less popular than indoor sports in the country. 化用了女方 not as popular as\nsome sports，但那是女方评普通排球、且对比对象是\"some sports\"而非\"室内运动\"，说\n话人和内容都对不上。\n借用原文：not as popular as some sports\nD) 偷换概念\nIt is completely different from other outdoor games. 几乎照搬男方的 completely\ndifferent 句式，唯独把比较对象从\"indoor volleyball（室内排球）\"偷换成\"other\noutdoor games（其他户外运动）\"——男方比的是室内外排球，不是和别的户外项目比。\n借用原文：beach volleyball and indoor volleyball are completely different", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about the lift-off of spacecraft?", "options": ["A) It usually requires years of preparations.", "B) It does a lot of harm to the environment.", "C) It often burns over three hundred tonnes of refined fuel.", "D) It proves to be the most complicated stage of space missions."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It does a lot of harm to the environment.\n【定位】Lift-off is usually the most environmentally harmful stage of any space mission, with vast\nquantities of fuel burnt up in a matter of minutes.\n发射升空通常是任何太空任务中对环境危害最大的阶段，几分钟内就有大量燃料被烧掉。\n【信号】篇章首句即点题。这是第一段的开篇第一句，篇章独白常把核心论断放在段首；句中 the\nmost environmentally harmful stage\"对环境危害最大的阶段\"是一个带最高级的明确论\n断，正是答案句信号。\n【替换】the most environmentally harmful stage ↔ does a lot of harm to the environment\n最高级换成动词短语\n【排除】A)\nIt usually requires years of preparations. 说发射\"通常需要数年准备\"，原文通篇没提准备\n时长，是凭\"航天听起来很复杂\"的常识臆造出来的。\nC)\nIt often burns over three hundred tonnes of refined fuel. 看似与原文 112 tonnes of\nrefined fuel 对得上，但原文说的 112 吨是\"精炼燃料\"，336 吨是排放的 CO₂；选项把\nover three hundred tonnes 安到了 refined fuel 头上，张冠李戴地偷换了数字。听到\nthree hundred 就选，正中陷阱。\n借用原文：Falcon 9 gets through 112 tonnes of refined fuel, emitting about 336 tonnes of CO₂\nD)\nIt proves to be the most complicated stage of space missions. 盗用了原文的最高级框\n架 the most environmentally harmful stage，但把 harmful（有害）偷换成\ncomplicated（复杂）。原文讲的是\"危害最大\"不是\"最复杂\"，听到 the most…stage 就\n选，会被这个偷换骗到。\n借用原文：the most environmentally harmful stage of any space mission", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What happens traditionally to boosters, fuel tanks and other components in spaceflight?", "options": ["A) They are burnt up in outer space.", "B) They are guided back to Earth.", "C) They are reused up to 100 times.", "D) They are treated as expendable."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are treated as expendable.\n【定位】Traditionally, boosters, fuel tanks and other components are treated as expendable.\n传统上，助推器、燃料箱和其他部件都被当作用完即弃的消耗品。\n【信号】限定词traditionally领出答案。句子以 Traditionally 开头，与题干里的 traditionally 完全\n对应；这种\"题干限定词在原文里原样出现\"的句子就是答案句。注意紧随其后的 But\nguiding them back to Earth 是转折后的新做法，问\"传统\"时不能抓那一句。\n【替换】treated as expendable ↔ They are treated as expendable.\n近乎原句照搬\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey are burnt up in outer space. 说部件\"在外太空被烧毁\"。原文第一段确有 fuel burnt\nup\"燃料被烧掉\"，但那是讲燃料、不是部件，更没说在外太空烧；这项盗用了 burnt up 的\n印象来骗人。\n借用原文：vast quantities of fuel burnt up in a matter of minutes\nB)\nThey are guided back to Earth. 对应原文 But guiding them back to Earth，但这是 But\n转折之后讲的\"新可能\"，是与\"传统\"相对的现在做法；题干问的是 traditionally，选 B 等于\n把转折后的新情况当成了传统做法。\n借用原文：But guiding them back to Earth in a controlled manner\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey are reused up to 100 times. 直接搬了原文 can be reused up to 100 times，原\n词、数字都对，极具迷惑性；但那同样是 But 转折之后的新做法，不是 traditionally 的传\n统处理。听到 100 times 就选，正中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：most components from the Falcon 9 can be reused up to 100 times", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the passage say we already have regarding spaceflight?", "options": ["A) New technologies for bringing back space vehicles for recycling.", "B) The technology for meeting the needs of commercial space travel.", "C) Many of the technologies to limit the impact of space travel on Earth.", "D) The technology for effectively protecting ozone from being destroyed."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Many of the technologies to limit the impact of space travel on Earth.\n【定位】But we already have many of the technologies needed to start limiting its impact on\nour planet.\n但我们已经拥有许多项所需的技术，可以开始限制太空旅行对地球的影响。\n【信号】尾段转折收束句。这是全文最后一段的收尾句，以 But we already have 起头：前一句\n说\"真正环保的太空旅行还很遥远\"，But 一转给出积极结论，转折之后才是文章真正落点，\n正是答案句。\n【替换】many of the technologies needed to start limiting its impact on our planet ↔\nMany of the technologies to limit the impact of space travel on Earth.\n保留核心仅压缩表述\n【排除】A)\nNew technologies for bringing back space vehicles for recycling. 借用了第三段\"回收\n复用\"的印象（guiding them back to Earth、reused），但原文尾句说的是\"已经拥有的\nmany of the technologies\"，不是 new technologies；且\"带回回收\"只是文中一个细节，\n拿它冒充全文结论，以偏概全。\n借用原文：guiding them back to Earth in a controlled manner\nB) 原词陷阱\nThe technology for meeting the needs of commercial space travel. 盗用了原文\ncommercial spaceflight\"商业航天\"这个词，但原文提它是说\"商业航天兴起让问题更紧\n迫\"，并非说\"我们已有满足商业航天需求的技术\"；这项把原词搬过来却换了说法，答非所\n问。\n借用原文：These issues are growing more pressing with the emergence of commercial spaceflight.\nD) 过度推断\nThe technology for effectively protecting ozone from being destroyed. 盗用了原文\nemit particles that destroy ozone 里的 ozone\"臭氧\"，但原文只是说火箭排放粒子\"会破\n坏臭氧\"，根本没说我们\"已有有效保护臭氧的技术\"；这项把一个细节问题夸大成现成的单项\n技术，无中生有。\n借用原文：rocket engines emit particles that destroy ozone", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does research show about reading to a loyal and nonjudgmental companion?", "options": ["A) It can help unskilled readers feel more secure.", "B) It can encourage reluctant readers to read aloud.", "C) It can strengthen children's confidence in others.", "D) It can boost children's interest in communication."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It can encourage reluctant readers to read aloud.\n【定位】Research shows that reading to a loyal and nonjudgmental companion, such as a dog or cat,\ncan encourage reluctant readers to read aloud, which will in turn boost their self-confidence.\n研究表明，读给一个忠诚且不加评判的伙伴（比如一只狗或猫）听，能鼓励那些不情愿读书\n的孩子大声朗读，这反过来又会增强他们的自信。\n【信号】题干原词复现处即答案句。录音里 Research shows that reading to a loyal and\nnonjudgmental companion 与题干用词几乎一字不差，题干问的对象在录音里原样出现，\n这句就是答案句；紧跟其后的 can encourage reluctant readers to read aloud 正是\"研究\n表明的结果\"。\n【替换】can encourage reluctant readers to read aloud ↔\nIt can encourage reluctant readers to read aloud.\n近乎原句搬运\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项 help unskilled readers feel more secure 里的 secure 来自录音后一句 help\nchildren feel secure，但那句讲的是\"有个伙伴倾诉感受\"带来的安全感，不是\"读给宠物\n听\"的研究结果；而且录音说的是 reluctant readers（不情愿的读者），A 偷换成 unskilled\nreaders（不熟练的读者）。\n借用原文：can also help children feel secure and develop communication skills\nC) 偷换概念\nC 项 strengthen children's confidence in others 借用了录音的 confidence，但录音说的\n是 boost their self-confidence（自信，对自己的信心），C 偷偷改成\"对他人的信心\n（confidence in others）\"，方向反了。\n借用原文：which will in turn boost their self-confidence\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 boost children's interest in communication 的 communication 来自录音末句\ndevelop communication skills，但那是\"有人倾诉感受\"的好处，不是\"读给宠物听\"的研究\n结果；而且录音说的是\"发展沟通技能\"，不是\"提升沟通兴趣\"。\n借用原文：develop communication skills", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "How can parents make pets a fantastic subject for their children's research and learning?", "options": ["A) By motivating children to find out about their favourite pets.", "B) By taking children to the library to borrow books on animals.", "C) By allowing children to surf the internet about pets by themselves.", "D) By showing children how to acquire knowledge about healthy pets."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） By motivating children to find out about their favourite pets.\n【定位】Parents can encourage their child to research their favourite pets at the library or during a\nsupervised internet search.\n父母可以鼓励孩子在图书馆或在有人监督的网络搜索中，去研究他们最喜欢的宠物。\n【信号】题干research落点处即答案句。题干问\"research and learning\"，录音第 3 段开头点题\nPets also provide a fantastic subject for research and learning，紧接着 Parents can\nencourage their child to research their favourite pets 就是父母做法的答案句，subject\nfor research 与题干完全对应。\n【替换】encourage their child to research their favourite pets ↔\nmotivating children to find out about their favourite pets\n同义词替换encourage=motivate、research=find out\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 项 taking children to the library to borrow books on animals 盗用了录音的 library，\n但录音说的是 research their favourite pets at the library（在图书馆研究宠物），是\"去\n图书馆查\"，并非\"带孩子去借书（borrow books）\"，borrow books on animals 是录音\n没说的。\n借用原文：to research their favourite pets at the library\nC) 与原文相反\nC 项 allowing children to surf the internet about pets by themselves 借用了录音的\ninternet，但录音原文是 a supervised internet search（有人监督的网络搜索），C 把它\n改成\"让孩子自己（by themselves）上网\"，恰恰与\"有人监督\"相反。\n借用原文：during a supervised internet search\nD) 偷换概念\nD 项 showing children how to acquire knowledge about healthy pets 把录音里 their\nfavourite pets（最喜欢的宠物）偷换成 healthy pets（健康的宠物）；healthy 这个词出\n自录音别处 keeping their pet happy and healthy，被挪到这里制造干扰，且\"研究主\n题\"是 favourite pets 而非 healthy pets。\n借用原文：to research their favourite pets", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "How can children still benefit even when their family doesn't have a pet?", "options": ["A) By learning that raising pets can do much good.", "B) By watching how healthy pets are brought up.", "C) By hearing about their peers' passion for pets.", "D) By visiting friends who have household pets."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） By visiting friends who have household pets.\n【定位】And of course, if a child doesn't have a pet in their own household, they can still benefit from\nall of the above when visiting a friend who does.\n当然，如果一个孩子自己家里没有宠物，他们在拜访一位养宠物的朋友时，仍然能从上述所\n有方面获益。\n【信号】if加still的让步收尾句。录音末段用 if a child doesn't have a pet in their own\nhousehold, they can still benefit 这个\"假设没有宠物 + still 仍能受益\"的让步结构收尾，\n正好对应题干的 even when…doesn't have a pet，是典型的答案信号句。\n【替换】when visiting a friend who does ↔ By visiting friends who have household pets\n代动词does=have a pet展开\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nA 项 learning that raising pets can do much good 把录音的 benefit 含义改头换面，说\n成\"懂得养宠物有好处\"，但题干问的是\"没养宠物时怎样仍能受益\"，答案是\"去拜访朋友\"这\n个动作，A 只是泛泛重复\"养宠物有益\"的大主旨，答非所问。\n借用原文：a child can benefit greatly from involvement in bringing up a happy and healthy pet\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 项 watching how healthy pets are brought up 借了录音多处出现的 healthy pet /\nbringing up，但录音从没说\"观看（watching）宠物怎么养大\"，这个动作是凭原词拼造出\n来的，并非没宠物时的受益途径。\n借用原文：bringing up a happy and healthy pet\nC) 未提及\nC 项 hearing about their peers' passion for pets 里的 passion 来自录音第 3 段 their\npassion for learning flourishes，但那讲的是\"孩子对学习的热情\"，录音通篇没说\"听同龄\n人（peers）讲他们对宠物的热情\"，这个途径属于无中生有。\n借用原文：as their passion for learning flourishes", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What would the new bill in the Ohio legislature require public colleges and universities to do?", "options": ["A) Send would-be students a packet of information.", "B) Update regularly all information on their websites.", "C) Inform prospective students of their yearly enrollment.", "D) Answer questions raised by applicants and their parents."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Send would-be students a packet of information.\n【定位】A new bill in the Ohio legislature would require state public colleges and universities to send\nprospective students a packet of information.\n俄亥俄州议会的一项新法案将要求州立公立高校向准学生寄送一个信息包。\n【信号】信号是首句直陈\"require…to send\"。答案句是第一段开门见山的一句 A new bill in the\nOhio legislature would require state public colleges and universities to send\nprospective students a packet of information，require…to send 直接给出 \"要求做的动\n作\"，篇章独白常在首句点题，这句即落点。\n【替换】send prospective students a packet of information ↔\nSend would-be students a packet of information.\nprospective=would-be，几乎同义改写\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nUpdate regularly all information on their websites. 借了原文末段确有的 provide\nsimilar information on their websites 里 websites、information 的影子，但法案要求的\n是 \"寄信息包\"，不是 \"定期更新网站\"，听到 website 就选会中招。\n借用原文：provide similar information on their websites\nC) 未提及\nInform prospective students of their yearly enrollment. 借用了 prospective students\n这个原词，但信息包里列的是 costs of college, grants and scholarships 等，根本没有\n\"年度招生人数（yearly enrollment）\"，属凭空添加的内容。\nD) 未提及\nAnswer questions raised by applicants and their parents. 听起来合理，但录音从头到尾\n说的是学校 \"主动寄信息包\"，没有 \"回答申请者和家长提问\" 这一动作，是想当然的添加。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What can the state only do according to Adam Mathews?", "options": ["A) Oversee private institutions directly.", "B) Follow other states' example.", "C) Supervise all schools consistently.", "D) Regulate public institutions by law."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Regulate public institutions by law.\n【定位】He notes that the state can only regulate public institutions in this way, but he hopes private\ninstitutions in Ohio follow the lead of the state and post similar information.\n他指出，州只能以这种方式监管公立院校，但他希望俄亥俄的私立院校效仿州的做法、也发\n布类似信息。\n【信号】信号是带only的限定句+but转折。答案句 the state can only regulate public\ninstitutions in this way 自带 only 限定，正面回答 \"只能做什么\"；其后 but he hopes\nprivate institutions…follow 用 but 把私立摘出去，反衬出 \"只能管公立\"，这种 only +\nbut 的结构就是答案信号。\n【替换】the state can only regulate public institutions in this way ↔ Regulate public institutions by law.\n\"by a new bill\"概括为\"by law\"\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nOversee private institutions directly. 抓的是 but 后面 private institutions in Ohio 这个\n词，但原文是 \"只能管公立、私立只是希望它效仿\"，把 \"管公立\" 偷换成 \"直接管私立\"，方\n向正好反。\n借用原文：but he hopes private institutions in Ohio follow the lead\nB) 张冠李戴\nFollow other states' example. 里 follow…lead 听着像原文 follow the lead of the\nstate，但原文是 \"私立效仿本州\"，不是 \"本州效仿别的州\"，且主语张冠李戴。\n借用原文：private institutions in Ohio follow the lead of the state\nC) 偷换概念\nSupervise all schools consistently. 用 all schools 把范围扩到所有学校，但答案句明确\ncan only regulate public institutions，only 把范围卡死在公立，all 与 only 直接矛盾。\n借用原文：the state can only regulate public institutions", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What do we learn about Ohio's schools from the passage?", "options": ["A) They are resourceful.", "B) They are competitive.", "C) They provide the best opportunity to realize one's dream.", "D) They appeal most to students from other states in the U.S."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They are competitive.\n【定位】Ohio's schools are already competitive. The state attracts more undergraduate students than it\nsends to colleges out of state.\n俄亥俄的学校已经很有竞争力了。该州吸引来的本科生比它送往外州读大学的还要多。\n【信号】信号是直陈定性句\"are already competitive\"。答案句 Ohio's schools are already\ncompetitive 是直接给学校下定义的一句，主系表把 competitive 这个定性词摆在明处；后\n一句 attracts more undergraduate students than it sends 用数据印证它，这种 \"先下结\n论再举证\" 的结构里，结论句即落点。\n【替换】Ohio's schools are already competitive ↔ They are competitive.\ncompetitive原词对应，仅换主语为They\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey are resourceful. 的 resourceful（资源丰富）听着像也是夸学校的好词，但录音夸学\n校用的是 competitive，并没说 resourceful，是凭空换了个褒义词。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey provide the best opportunity to realize one's dream. 整句几乎照搬第二段 give\nthem the best opportunity to match their skills and their dreams，但原文那是议员说\n\"把信息摆给学生能给他们最好的机会\"，主语是 \"提供信息\"，不是说学校本身就是 \"实现梦\n想的最佳机会\"，且本题问的是 \"了解到学校什么\"。\n借用原文：give them the best opportunity to match their skills and their dreams\nD) 偷换概念\nThey appeal most to students from other states in the U.S. 借了第三段 attracts more\nundergraduate students than it sends to colleges out of state，但原文说的是 \"吸引来\n的本科生比送出去的多\"，并非 \"对外州学生最有吸引力\"，appeal most 是夸大且改了意\n思。\n借用原文：attracts more undergraduate students than it sends to colleges out of state", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What is the problem with schools providing information on their websites?", "options": ["A) Some students waste much time surfing the net.", "B) Students may need to visit too many websites.", "C) Students have to search for the information.", "D) Some students may lack adequate access."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Students have to search for the information.\n【定位】Many colleges and universities currently provide similar information on their websites.\nThis includes tuition, fees, graduation rates and more. But students need to search for\nthe information.\n许多高校目前已在网站上提供类似信息，包括学费、各项收费、毕业率等等。但学生得自己\n去搜索这些信息。\n【信号】信号是But转折后的短板句。前面 Many colleges and universities currently provide\nsimilar information on their websites 在夸网站信息已经很全，But students need to\nsearch for the information 用 But 一转点出 \"得自己找\" 的短板——But 后面这句才是 \"问\n题\" 所在，正是答案句。\n【替换】students need to search for the information ↔ Students have to search for the information.\nneed to=have to，近乎原句\n【排除】A) 未提及\nSome students waste much time surfing the net. 借了 \"网络\" 的话题氛围，但原文只说\n学生 \"得自己搜\"，没说 \"浪费很多时间上网\"，waste much time 是凭空添加的。\nB) 原词陷阱\nStudents may need to visit too many websites. 抓住原文 on their websites 的\nwebsites 一词，把 \"得自己搜信息\" 夸张成 \"得访问太多网站\"，原文从没说网站 too\nmany。\n借用原文：provide similar information on their websites\nD) 未提及\nSome students may lack adequate access. 说学生 \"缺少足够渠道\"，但原文的问题是 \"信\n息在网上、得自己去搜\"，并不是 \"上不了网/没渠道\"，access 这层意思原文没有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adaptable", "B) closed", "C) distribution", "D) interact", "E) narration", "F) neutral", "G) permanently", "H) prescribes", "I) readily", "J) registered", "K) reinforces", "L) revealed", "M) significant", "N) specific", "O) speculate"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) revealed\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 New research 后缺谓语，需要单数动词\n【依据】research revealed that... 是科研报道高频搭配\n句子是典型的\"新研究揭示……\"句式，New research from biologists at the University of\nMaryland __(26)__ that sight deprivation also changes the way brain cells __(27)__\nwith one another，research revealed/showed that 是四级和考研阅读里科普类语料最常\n见的引出研究结论的搭配。\n【竞争词】H) prescribes 义为\"开处方/规定\"，与\"研究揭示\"不沾边\nprescribe 多用于医生开药或权威机构\"规定\"，与 research 做主语完全不搭，且其内容 that 从\n句是研究结论，不是规定。\nK) reinforces 义为\"加强\"，需要被加强的对象，与本句\"首次揭示\"不符\nreinforce 是\"加强、强化已有事物\"，常见结构 reinforces the idea/finding/argument\nthat...；本句是直接给出\"新研究\"的发现，并非在加强某个先前结论，更适合放在第 28 空。\nO) speculate 原形动词，主谓不一致；且语气是\"推测\"不是\"揭示\"\nspeculate 是原形，主语 New research 为三单需要 -s 形式，形态先不符；语义上\"推测\"也弱\n于\"揭示\"，与前句已知事实并列叙述的语气不匹配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adaptable", "B) closed", "C) distribution", "D) interact", "E) narration", "F) neutral", "G) permanently", "H) prescribes", "I) readily", "J) registered", "K) reinforces", "L) revealed", "M) significant", "N) specific", "O) speculate"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) interact\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 brain cells 后做谓语，需原形动词\n【依据】interact with one another = \"相互作用\"，神经科学固定搭配\nchanges the way brain cells __(27)__ with one another 描述的是脑细胞之间的相互作用\n方式，interact with one another\"彼此相互作用\"是生物/神经科学的标准说法，与全文主\n旨\"剥夺视觉改变脑细胞间互动\"完美对应。\n【竞争词】H) prescribes 三单 -es 形态与复数主语 brain cells 冲突\nprescribes 是三单形式，主语是复数 brain cells，主谓数不一致；且语义\"开处方/规定\"也与脑\n细胞行为不搭。\nK) reinforces 三单形态错 + 语义需要宾语\nreinforces 一是三单形态不匹配复数主语；二是 reinforce 是及物动词需要直接宾语，本句空后\n是 with one another 而非名词宾语，搭配不成立。\nO) speculate 义为\"推测\"，脑细胞不会\"推测\"\nspeculate 是有意识主体的认知行为，与无意识的 brain cells 主语在语义上违和，且 speculate\nwith 也不是常用搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adaptable", "B) closed", "C) distribution", "D) interact", "E) narration", "F) neutral", "G) permanently", "H) prescribes", "I) readily", "J) registered", "K) reinforces", "L) revealed", "M) significant", "N) specific", "O) speculate"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) reinforces\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 This study 后缺谓语，需要三单形式\n【依据】study reinforces what we are learning =\"研究强化/印证了我们的认识\"\n上一段已经讲了\"剥夺视觉影响脑细胞相互作用\"，本句作者引述研究者评价\"这项研究 ___ 我\n们正在学到的东西\"，逻辑上是\"再次印证/强化\"既有认识，reinforces what we are\nlearning 搭配地道，This study __(28)__ what we are learning about how manipulating\nvision can have a __(29)__ effect on the ability of an animal to hear。\n【竞争词】H) prescribes 义为\"开处方/规定\"，与\"印证研究发现\"完全不搭\nprescribe 用于医生开药或权威给出规定，主语一般是人或机构，且不与 what we are learning\n这种\"我们正在学到的东西\"搭配，语义荒谬。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— prescribes 形态对、三单一致，主体考生不认词义时易\n蒙。This study __(28)__ what we are learning 处，prescribes 与正确答案 reinforces 在\n形态上都是三单 -es，许多四级考生对 prescribe 只记得\"开处方\"半个义项，会怀疑\"是不是\n有别的意思\"而硬选；防坑要点是看主语 study 和宾语 what we are learning——\"研究\"只\n会\"印证/强化\"，不会\"规定\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adaptable", "B) closed", "C) distribution", "D) interact", "E) narration", "F) neutral", "G) permanently", "H) prescribes", "I) readily", "J) registered", "K) reinforces", "L) revealed", "M) significant", "N) specific", "O) speculate"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) significant\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 与名词 effect 之间，要填形容词作定语\n【依据】搭配 have a significant effect on 是四级高频固定表达\nhave a significant effect on \"对……有显著影响\" 是四级阅读和写作里反复出现的高频搭\n配，配合上下文 manipulating vision can have a __(29)__ effect on the ability of an\nanimal to hear，作者要强调操控视觉对听觉能力的影响\"重大\"，significant 正是这层\n意思。\n【竞争词】A) adaptable 义为\"可适应的\"，与 effect 不搭\nadaptable 通常修饰人或系统（如 an adaptable learner），说\"一个可适应的影响\"在中英文里\n都不通顺，搭配错位。\nF) neutral \"中立的影响\"在科学语境里反向且无意义\nneutral effect 表示\"既不正也不负、没什么作用\"，但作者全篇都在强调剥夺视觉\"确实改变\n了\"听觉皮层，正话反说，与主旨冲突。\nN) specific 义为\"特定的\"，文中并未限定某一种影响\nspecific effect 强调\"某一具体的影响\"，需要后文点明是哪一种；而本句只是泛指\"有影响\"，逻\n辑上不需要这种限定，且 specific 在第 33 空有更典型的归属（specific frequencies）。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adaptable", "B) closed", "C) distribution", "D) interact", "E) narration", "F) neutral", "G) permanently", "H) prescribes", "I) readily", "J) registered", "K) reinforces", "L) revealed", "M) significant", "N) specific", "O) speculate"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) closed\n动词\n【词性槽】have to have + ___，需要过去分词，构成完成时态被动表达\n【依据】the window has closed =\"窗口已关闭\"，比喻关键期结束\n原文用 the window for auditory learning 比喻\"听觉学习的关键期\"，long after the\nwindow for auditory learning was thought to have __(30)__ 说\"这个窗口被认为早已关\n闭\"，对应下段第 31 空所讨论的\"过了关键期后不可塑\"的传统观点；window has closed\n是\"关键期/机会窗口已关\"的固定比喻。\n【竞争词】J) registered 义为\"登记\"，与\"窗口关闭\"语义无关\nregistered 多用于\"注册、登记、记录在案\"，搭配 the window 在英语里没有对应的固定比喻，\n意思完全跑偏。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adaptable", "B) closed", "C) distribution", "D) interact", "E) narration", "F) neutral", "G) permanently", "H) prescribes", "I) readily", "J) registered", "K) reinforces", "L) revealed", "M) significant", "N) specific", "O) speculate"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) adaptable\n形容词\n【词性槽】系动词 were 后做表语\n【依据】下一段反驳\"不可塑\"，正好对应 adaptable\n本段是先立后破：先说\"曾经认为感觉区在童年关键期后就不再 ___\"，紧接着 Kanold's\nearlier research disproved this idea by showing that depriving adult mice of vision\nfor a short period increased the sensitivity of individual neurons in the auditory\ncortex 用 disproved 反驳，证明成年后大脑仍可被改变。adaptable\"可塑、可适应\"恰好是\n被反驳的那种属性，否定的就是\"不可塑\"。\n【竞争词】F) neutral neutral 与上下文论辩焦点无关\n上下文的对立面是\"可塑 vs 固定\"，不是\"中性 vs 偏向\"；填 neutral 后整句变成\"曾认为大脑感觉\n区不是中性的\"，与下文用 disproved 反驳的语义完全对不上。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— neutral 词性、形态都对，迷惑性强。the sensory regions\nof the brain were not __(31)__ 处 be 动词后填形容词，A 和 F 都是合格形容词；考生若不\n抓住下句 disproved 的反驳逻辑，容易被 neutral 的\"中性、不偏不倚\"误导；防坑靠回看下\n一句反驳的是什么。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adaptable", "B) closed", "C) distribution", "D) interact", "E) narration", "F) neutral", "G) permanently", "H) prescribes", "I) readily", "J) registered", "K) reinforces", "L) revealed", "M) significant", "N) specific", "O) speculate"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) readily\n副词\n【词性槽】修饰动词 learn，且夹在 more 比较级里\n【依据】readily =\"容易地、轻松地\"，对应\"小孩学语言比成人快\"的常识\n全段强调\"成年大脑感觉区曾被认为不再可塑\"，这是为什么 children learn languages\nmuch more __(32)__ than adults——比较的是\"学得容易、学得快\"，readily\"轻易地、迅\n速地\"正中靶心。\n【竞争词】G) permanently 义为\"永久地\"，与\"比成人……\"的比较语义不搭\npermanently 强调\"持续不断\"，但语境对比的是儿童 vs 成人\"学语言这件事做得怎么样\"，需要\n的是\"快 / 容易\"，不是\"持久\"；填 permanently 后整句变成\"小孩比成人更永久地学语言\"，逻辑\n混乱。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adaptable", "B) closed", "C) distribution", "D) interact", "E) narration", "F) neutral", "G) permanently", "H) prescribes", "I) readily", "J) registered", "K) reinforces", "L) revealed", "M) significant", "N) specific", "O) speculate"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) specific\n形容词\n【词性槽】形容词修饰复数名词 frequencies\n【依据】specific frequencies = \"特定频率\"，正中神经科学语境\n上下文说\"幼年大脑根据常听到的声音连线，把听觉皮层的区域分配给\"___ 的频率，意思\n是\"对应到某些特定的频率\"，specific frequencies 是听觉/神经科学论文中的高频搭配，意\n思贴合 assigning areas of the auditory cortex to __(33)__ frequencies based on what\nthey are used to hearing。\n【竞争词】A) adaptable \"可适应的频率\"逻辑不通\n频率本身是物理量，不存在\"可不可适应\"，adaptable 通常修饰生命体或系统，搭配在常识层面\n就不成立。\nF) neutral \"中性频率\"与上下文\"基于听到的声音\"逻辑相反\nneutral 强调\"无偏好\"，但句子明说大脑是\"基于它们常听到的声音\"做分配，恰恰是有偏好、有\n针对性的分配，方向相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adaptable", "B) closed", "C) distribution", "D) interact", "E) narration", "F) neutral", "G) permanently", "H) prescribes", "I) readily", "J) registered", "K) reinforces", "L) revealed", "M) significant", "N) specific", "O) speculate"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) distribution\n名词\n【词性槽】the ___ of space to different frequencies，定冠词 the 后 of 短语前的名词位\n【依据】distribution of space to frequencies =\"空间向不同频率的分配\"\n上文 Young brains wire themselves according to the sounds they hear frequently,\nassigning areas of the auditory cortex to __(33)__ frequencies based on what they\nare used to hearing. 刚说幼年大脑把皮层区域\"分配给\"特定频率，本句紧跟着说成年大鼠\n经过一周黑暗后也改变了\"空间对不同频率的分配\"，distribution of … to … 与上文\nassigning … to … 概念呼应，搭配地道。\n【竞争词】E) narration 义为\"叙述\"，与神经科学语境无关\nnarration 用于讲述故事、电影旁白等，本文是讨论听觉皮层的空间分配，narration of space\nto frequencies 在语义上完全跑题。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adaptable", "B) closed", "C) distribution", "D) interact", "E) narration", "F) neutral", "G) permanently", "H) prescribes", "I) readily", "J) registered", "K) reinforces", "L) revealed", "M) significant", "N) specific", "O) speculate"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) speculate\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 We 后缺谓语，原形动词作一般现在时\n【依据】speculate that =\"推测\"，与从句的 may have to do with 完美呼应\n从句里有 may，表明说话人不下定论，主句的动词应同样表\"推测、猜测\"，We __(35)__\nthat it may have to do with what the mice are paying attention to while they are in\nthe dark. 整句意思是\"我们推测这可能与小鼠在黑暗中注意什么有关\"，speculate 既贴合语\n气又契合上下文。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Judging from the slowing rate of sales growth, there is still hope to combat bottled water.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】销售增速放缓\n【定位】The rate of growth has begun to ease (sales were up 7% in the year, compared with 8% the\nprevious year).\n增速已经开始放缓（本年度销售增长 7%，比前一年的 8% 有所下降）。\n【改写】the slowing rate of sales growth ↔ The rate of growth has begun to ease\nslowing 与 has begun to ease 同义\nthere is still hope to combat bottled water ↔ Hope is not entirely out of reach\n双重否定等于肯定\n【辨邻】 B B 段的重点是 Sales in the UK were worth a record £558.4 million this past year,\nan increase of 7%，强调“销售在涨”，没有 slowing 这层意思，也没有 there is still\nhope 这一对照，所以只是话题相邻、并非源头段。\nSales in the UK were worth a record £558.4 million this past year, an increase of 7%", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Bottled water manufacturers base their arguments against bans of plastic bottles on health concerns rather than on profits.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】厂商以健康为由反对禁令\n【定位】But they raise concerns about health rather than profit margins.\n但他们打的是健康牌，而不是利润牌。\n【改写】base their arguments against bans of plastic bottles on health concerns rather than on profits ↔\nthey raise concerns about health rather than profit margins\nrather than 对照结构等值\nBottled water manufacturers ↔ Water bottlers\n同义近义词指同一对象", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Sales of bottled water in Britain hit a record high last year even though people are increasingly aware that plastics are environmentally unfriendly.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】英国销售创纪录\n【定位】Sales in the UK were worth a record £558.4 million this past year, an increase of 7%.\n英国本年度的销售额达到了创纪录的 5.584 亿英镑，同比增长 7%。\n【改写】Sales of bottled water in Britain hit a record high last year ↔\nSales in the UK were worth a record £558.4 million this past year\nrecord 对 record 加 UK 对 Britain\npeople are increasingly aware that plastics are environmentally unfriendly ↔\nIt defies our increasing awareness of the harm plastics do to the environment\nincreasing awareness 原词复现\n【辨邻】 E E 段重点是 The rate of growth has begun to ease (sales were up 7% in the year,\ncompared with 8% the previous year)，强调“在变慢”；陈述句要的是 hit a record\nhigh，方向相反，因此 E 不是源头。\nThe rate of growth has begun to ease (sales were up 7% in the year, compared with 8% the\nprevious year)", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "It often happens that people can lack reason when faced with skillful marketing.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】营销压过理性\n【定位】As is so often the case, clever marketing can beat reason; awareness is rarely enough.\n正如经常发生的那样，聪明的营销能压过理性；仅有意识远远不够。\n【改写】people can lack reason when faced with skillful marketing ↔ clever marketing can beat reason\n视角翻转的等值改写\nIt often happens that ↔ As is so often the case\n频度状语同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "One city on the west coast of America has banned single-use bottles from its property and events.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】美国西海岸某城禁瓶\n【定位】San Francisco has banned them from city property and events.\n旧金山已经禁止在市政府的房产和活动中提供这类瓶装水。\n【改写】One city on the west coast of America ↔ San Francisco\n具体城市对上位地理描述\nbanned single-use bottles from its property and events ↔\nhas banned them from city property and events\n几乎逐词复现\n【辨邻】 D D 段重点是 Moves against various plastics have gathered pace，是宏观趋势，没\n有点名美国城市，与陈述句要找的“西海岸某城禁令”在颗粒度上不匹配。\nMoves against various plastics have gathered pace, from shopping bags to straws and plastic-lined\ncoffee cups", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Manufacturing and shipping of plastic water bottles consume a tremendous amount of fossil fuels.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】化石燃料消耗\n【定位】Plastic water bottles require oceans of fossil fuels to make and ship.\n塑料水瓶的生产和运输需要消耗海量的化石燃料。\n【改写】Manufacturing and shipping of plastic water bottles ↔\nPlastic water bottles require oceans of fossil fuels to make and ship\n动词不定式对动名词\nconsume a tremendous amount of fossil fuels ↔ require oceans of fossil fuels\n夸张量词同义替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "One large beverage company has adapted its operations when confronted with challenges from health and environmental advocates.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】一家大饮料公司因双压力做调整\n【定位】As the owner of multiple sugary drink brands and bottled water, PepsiCo is facing challenges\non health and environmental fronts.\n百事可乐作为多个含糖饮料品牌和瓶装水的母公司，正面临着来自健康和环保两个方面的\n挑战。\n【改写】One large beverage company ↔ PepsiCo\n具体公司对应上位描述\nadapted its operations ↔\nthe company bought SodaStream (a drinks company that sells machines for making tap water\nbubbly and then consumers add flavours) for $3.2 billion\n动作概括对具体举措\nchallenges from health and environmental advocates ↔\nfacing challenges on health and environmental fronts\n原词复现\n【辨邻】 H H 段主语是反对禁令的瓶装水厂商，重点是 they raise concerns about health\nrather than profit margins——他们打健康牌反对禁令，而非“主动调整”。陈述句要\n的是“一家大公司被双压力推着做调整”，方向不符。\nthey raise concerns about health rather than profit margins", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Bottled water is considerably more expensive than tap water.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】瓶装水比自来水贵很多\n【定位】A litre of tap water, the stuff we have cleverly piped into our homes, costs less than half a\npenny. A litre of bottled water can cost well over a pound, especially for something fancy that\nhas been sucked through a mountain.\n一升自来水——也就是我们巧妙地通过管道引入家中的那种水——花费不到半便士。一升瓶\n装水却可能要花掉一英镑多，尤其是那种被吹嘘“从山中取水”的高端货。\n【改写】Bottled water is considerably more expensive than tap water ↔\nA litre of bottled water can cost well over a pound\n数字对照即比较", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Fountains could be seen in cities before bottled water became popular.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】瓶装水流行前城市里有饮水龙头\n【定位】Before plastic and the marketing that made people think they needed bottled water in the first\nplace, fountains were an urban fixture.\n在塑料和那种让人们以为自己需要瓶装水的营销出现之前，饮水龙头就是城市里的标配。\n【改写】before bottled water became popular ↔\nBefore plastic and the marketing that made people think they needed bottled water in the first\nplace\n营销让人觉得需要等于流行\nFountains could be seen in cities ↔ fountains were an urban fixture\n都市标配等于在城市随处可见", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "More people have taken to bottled water because of their health awareness.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】因健康意识转向瓶装水\n【定位】The introduction of the \"sugar tax\" on juices and soda drinks has pushed more people to\nbottled water, while health awareness has boosted its desirability.\n对果汁和苏打饮料征收的“糖税”，把更多人推向了瓶装水；与此同时，健康意识的提升也增\n强了瓶装水的吸引力。\n【改写】More people have taken to bottled water ↔ has pushed more people to bottled water\n动词同义替换\nbecause of their health awareness ↔ health awareness has boosted its desirability\n原因状语对主谓因果\n【辨邻】 H H 段重点是 they raise concerns about health rather than profit margins——是厂\n商用健康话术反对禁令；陈述句要的是消费者因健康意识转向瓶装水，施动者与情节都\n不符。\nthey raise concerns about health rather than profit margins", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What has the author come to realise since entering university?", "options": ["A) He needs a crash course on financial terms.", "B) He is very much lacking in financial literacy.", "C) It requires consistent education to be financially independent.", "D) It is unrealistic to give all Australian youth a financial education."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What has the author come to realise直接问一个具体认知结果，不含infer/imply/mainly\nabout等推断或主旨信号词。\n【拆句】What has the author come to realise since entering university?主干是What ... has\ncome to realise，What作realise的宾语；since entering university是时间状语，结构平\n铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】As a university student, I've come to realise just how little I know about money.\n作为一名大学生，我已经意识到自己对金钱了解得有多么少。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nHe needs a crash course on financial terms说作者需要一门速成课，而原文Diving\nheadfirst into the crash course of starting university里的crash course指的是大学开\n学这件事本身像速成课，不是金融术语速成课。\nB) ✓ 正确\njust how little I know about money ↔ lacking in financial literacy\n预判说我钱的知识少，He is very much lacking in financial literacy里的lacking in\nfinancial literacy正是这层意思的标准表达。\nC) 答非所问\nIt requires consistent education to be financially independent谈的是要做到\nfinancially independent的条件，而题干问的是作者意识到什么，P1里realise的宾语并\n不是这个条件论。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt is unrealistic to give all Australian youth a financial education说给澳洲青年金融\n教育不现实，而原文Australia's youth are being done a great disservice by not\nreceiving any consistent financial education恰恰是在痛心他们没有得到金融教育，\n作者立场是该给而不是不现实。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "How did the author feel in today's money world?", "options": ["A) Badly equipped to survive.", "B) Ignorant of financial literature.", "C) Barely capable of moving ahead.", "D) Overwhelmed by the resources online."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How did the author feel直接问感受，是关于感受这个事实的细节题，不含推断信号词。\n【拆句】How did the author feel in today's money world?主干How did the author feel，in\ntoday's money world是地点/情境状语；结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】I felt that I was sailing the financial seas with no skills and no life jacket!\n我感觉自己正在金融的海洋上航行，却既没有技能也没有救生衣！\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nsailing the financial seas with no skills and no life jacket ↔ Badly equipped to survive\n预判说作者缺装备处境危险，Badly equipped to survive里badly equipped to\nsurvive正是这层意思的标准等值表达。\nB) 偷换概念\nIgnorant of financial literature里financial literature是金融文献，而原文谈的是\nfinancial literacy/skills，文献和素养是两回事，选项偷换了对象。\nC) 程度副词偷换\nBarely capable of moving ahead说勉强能前进，原文no skills and no life jacket描绘\n的是完全无装备的处境，是不能前进而不是勉强能。\nD) 答非所问\nOverwhelmed by the resources online说被网络资源压垮，而原文hundreds of great\nresources out there并没有说作者被压垮，反而是肯定资源很多，作者的负面感受来自\n缺装备不是资源多。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What did the author realise after talking to his friends?", "options": ["A) They were as keen as he was on financial matters.", "B) The schooling system was to blame for his trouble.", "C) High schoolers knew nothing about the modern financial world.", "D) Financial courses were as unpopular as compulsory English classes."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What did the author realise after talking to his friends问的是一个具体认知结果，没有\ninfer/imply等信号词。\n【拆句】What did the author realise after talking to his friends?主干What did the author\nrealise，after talking to his friends是时间状语；结构平直，无陷阱。\n【定位】However, after talking to my friends who felt the same, I quickly realised that it wasn't my\nignorance but the lack of financial education in our schooling system that is leaving high\nschoolers seriously behind in the modern world of money.\n然而，和有同感的朋友们聊过之后，我很快意识到，让高中生在现代金钱世界里严重落后\n的，不是我的无知，而是我们学校系统里金融教育的缺失。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nThey were as keen as he was on financial matters说朋友们也对金融问题热心，而原\n文my friends who felt the same只说朋友们有同感（同样觉得吃力），并没说他们热\n心研究金融。\nB) ✓ 正确\nnot...but the lack of financial education in our schooling system ↔\nThe schooling system was to blame for his trouble\n预判说学校系统背锅，The schooling system was to blame for his trouble里was to\nblame正是这层归因意思。\nC) 以偏概全\nHigh schoolers knew nothing about the modern financial world说高中生对金融世\n界一无所知，而原文leaving high schoolers seriously behind说的是严重落后不是一\n无所知，knew nothing是把落后扩大成零知识。\nD) 无中生有\nFinancial courses were as unpopular as compulsory English classes说金融课和必\n修英语一样不受欢迎，原文English may not be the most popular subject只是承认英\n语不算最受欢迎，从未说金融课不受欢迎或两者并列，且这点出现在48题区间之外，纯\n属无中生有。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is the author's idea of a financial education course?", "options": ["A) It should foresee students' needs after graduation.", "B) It should provide students with some basic knowledge.", "C) It should be taught the same way as English is taught.", "D) It should be integrated into high school education."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is the author's idea of a financial education course?问的是作者明确提出的具体主\n张，原文应有should句直接对应，不需推断。\n【拆句】What is the author's idea of a financial education course?主干What is the author's\nidea，of短语限定idea的对象，结构平直无陷阱。\n【定位】It shouldn't be just a one-day event but a course integrated throughout the whole of high\nschool that would allow students to gradually expand their financial literacy, and would\nprevent the need for a 'catch up' phase once we're out on our own after graduation.\n它不该只是一个一日活动，而该是一门贯穿整个高中阶段的课程，让学生逐步拓展自己的金\n融素养，从而避免我们毕业独立生活后还得补课。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nIt should foresee students' needs after graduation说该课该预见学生毕业后的需\n求，而原文prevent the need for a 'catch up' phase once we're out on our own\nafter graduation说的是避免毕业后补课，不是预见学生需求，foresee needs偷换了\nprevent the need这层因果。\nB) 以偏概全\nIt should provide students with some basic knowledge说该课只该提供基础知识，\n而原文gradually expand their financial literacy说的是逐步拓展金融素养，且years to\ngradually accumulate bits and pieces of knowledge强调长期积累，some basic把整\n个长期拓展过程缩成基础知识，是范围偷小。\nC) 过度推断\nIt should be taught the same way as English is taught说该课该用和英语一样的教\n法，而原文Let's compare a theoretical financial education subject to the standard\ncompulsory English class只是拿英语做类比强调持续性，并没有说要在教法上完全一\n样，taught the same way是把类比推过头。\nD) ✓ 正确\na course integrated throughout the whole of high school ↔\nIt should be integrated into high school education\n预判说该课应融入高中始终，It should be integrated into high school education里\nintegrated into high school education正是这层意思的同义表达。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What would financial literacy do to young people?", "options": ["A) Allow them to enter adulthood with financial security.", "B) Enable them to look after themselves without worrying about money.", "C) Render them confident and secure in terms of money management.", "D) Help them become familiar with the world of money."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What would financial literacy do to young people问的是金融素养对年轻人的具体作\n用，原文应有直接对应句。\n【拆句】What would financial literacy do to young people?主干What would financial literacy\ndo，to young people是间接宾语；结构平直无陷阱。\n【定位】Giving young people the opportunity to become familiar with the world of money would\nprovide them with a great advantage to enter adulthood with confidence and security so that\nthey are able to manage their own money and look after themselves.\n让年轻人有机会熟悉金钱世界，会让他们带着自信和安全感步入成年，并因此能够自己管理\n钱财、照顾好自己。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nAllow them to enter adulthood with financial security只保留了security丢掉了\nconfidence，且把confidence and security压缩为financial security一项，原文是双重\n收益而不是只有金融安全。\nB) 过度推断\nEnable them to look after themselves without worrying about money里without\nworrying about money是说不必为钱发愁，而原文able to manage their own\nmoney只说能管理钱，不等于不必为钱发愁，without worrying是从能管理钱推过头。\nC) ✓ 正确\nenter adulthood with confidence and security so that they are able to manage their own money\n↔ Render them confident and secure in terms of money management\n预判说在金钱管理上自信且有安全感，Render them confident and secure in terms\nof money management里confident and secure in terms of money management\n正是这层意思最完整的同义替换。\nD) 答非所问\nHelp them become familiar with the world of money说帮他们熟悉金钱世界，而原\n文Giving young people the opportunity to become familiar with the world of\nmoney里熟悉金钱世界是金融素养这件事的前提条件而不是它带来的后果，题干问的是\nfinancial literacy带来什么，不是它是什么。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do people believe chocolates can do?", "options": ["A) Cheer them up instantly.", "B) Create happy calories.", "C) Conceal emotional distress.", "D) Relieve them of heart trouble."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do people believe chocolates can do? 问的是巧克力能干什么这一具体功能，没有\ninfer/imply/mainly 等信号词。\n【拆句】题干主干 What do people believe chocolates can do? 是一个嵌套宾语从句的特殊疑问\n句：What 是 do 的宾语，people 是主语，believe 后跟省略 that 的宾语从句 chocolates\ncan do (what)。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】We all have faith in chocolates to delight us in an instant!\n我们都相信巧克力能在一瞬间让我们开心起来！\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ndelight us in an instant ↔ Cheer them up instantly\n「瞬间让人开心」的预判与 Cheer them up instantly 在语义上完全重合，无差量，直\n接锁定。\nB) 偷换概念识别\nCreate happy calories 把原文 channelling happy calories inside us 中「输送/导入\n愉快卡路里」当成「创造卡路里」，把比喻动作偷换成 create。\nC) 与原文相反识别\n原文是 They comfort us in times of trouble——「安抚」情绪，而 Conceal\nemotional distress 说「隐藏」情绪，安抚≠隐藏，意思相反。\nD) 过度推断识别\n原文 calming down a racing heart 是比喻「让狂跳的心平静下来」，而 Relieve them\nof heart trouble 把 racing heart 强行解为「心脏疾病」并把 calming down 换成\nrelieve…of，属于过度推断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What was scientists' recent assertion about chocolates?", "options": ["A) They could become a rare treat in the near future.", "B) They could calm people down a bit in times of crisis.", "C) They could prevent people from getting heartbroken.", "D) They could become unavailable in less than 30 years."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What was scientists' recent assertion about chocolates? 问的是科学家断言的具体内\n容，原文中可定位一句即可作答。\n【拆句】题干 What was scientists' recent assertion about chocolates? 是 What…be 句型，主语\nscientists' recent assertion 由所有格 + 形容词 + 名词构成，about chocolates 是后置介\n词短语修饰 assertion。结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】Recently, chocolate lovers were heartbroken as scientists claimed that they can become\nextinct by 2050!\n最近，巧克力爱好者们伤心欲绝，因为科学家声称巧克力到 2050 年可能灭绝！\n【选项】A) 过度推断识别\nThey could become a rare treat in the near future 说巧克力会变成「稀有美食」，\n但原文是 extinct（灭绝/绝种），不是 rare（稀少），属于程度上的过度推断与偷换。\nB) 答非所问识别\nThey could calm people down a bit in times of crisis 描述的是巧克力的安抚功能，\n是首段的内容；题干问的是「科学家最近的断言」，不在第一段范围内。\nC) 与原文相反识别\nThey could prevent people from getting heartbroken 利用 chocolate lovers were\nheartbroken 中的 heartbroken 反向拼凑，把「伤心」错改为「防止伤心」，与原文方\n向完全相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthey can become extinct by 2050 ↔ They could become unavailable in less than 30 years\n「2050 年前灭绝」的预判与 They could become unavailable in less than 30 years\n完全重合，时间值与「灭绝/不可得」均对得上。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What would happen if the cacao farms were shifted to cooler mountainous areas?", "options": ["A) The natural habitat of wildlife there would be ruined.", "B) The cacao farmers would have a tough time to adapt.", "C) The rainforests would be shrinking dramatically.", "D) The quality of cocoa beans would suffer greatly."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What would happen if the cacao farms were shifted to cooler mountainous areas?\n是 if 条件下的客观结果，可直接到原文条件句中找到表述。\n【拆句】题干 What would happen if the cacao farms were shifted to cooler mountainous\nareas? 是 What + 虚拟条件句：主句 what would happen 用 would，从句 were shifted\n是被动语态，整体表达「假设农场被迁走，会发生什么」。要注意 were shifted 是被动结\n构，主体是农场而非农民。\n【定位】These farms will then have to be shifted to cooler mountainous areas, which are the natural\nhabitat of wildlife.\n这些农场届时将不得不被迁到更凉爽的山区，而那里正是野生动物的自然栖息地。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ncooler mountainous areas, which are the natural habitat of wildlife ↔\nThe natural habitat of wildlife there would be ruined\nwhether to grow chocolate or save wildlife ↔ would be ruined\n「侵占并破坏野生动物栖息地」的预判与 The natural habitat of wildlife there would\nbe ruined 完全重合。\nB) 无中生有识别\nThe cacao farmers would have a tough time to adapt 谈的是「农民适应困难」，原\n文从未提及农民的适应问题，纯属编造。\nC) 答非所问识别\nThe rainforests would be shrinking dramatically 谈的是雨林萎缩，但题干问的是\n「农场迁到凉爽山区之后」，迁出地（雨林）的变化并不是题干问的「会发生什么」的\n核心后果，且原文未说雨林会因此显著萎缩。\nD) 无中生有识别\nThe quality of cocoa beans would suffer greatly 说可可豆质量会大幅下降，原文从\n未谈及「质量」问题，只提到产量（yields）下降；属于无中生有。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What do we learn about the cacao farms in the crisis of global warming?", "options": ["A) They try to seek help from gene-editing scientists.", "B) They decide to move to cooler mountainous areas.", "C) They have suffered a lot due to a decrease in produce.", "D) They have benefited by raising prices of cocoa beans."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about the cacao farms in the crisis of global warming? 问的是文中\n告诉我们的客观事实，可在原文找一句直接陈述。\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn about the cacao farms in the crisis of global warming? 主干为\nWhat do we learn about Y，about 后接 the cacao farms in the crisis of global\nwarming，in 引导的介词短语充当 farms 的处境状语。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Unfortunately, the crisis of global warming has already had a serious negative impact on\ncacao farms' yields, leading to the prices of chocolates skyrocketing.\n不幸的是，全球变暖危机已经严重负面地影响了可可园的产量，导致巧克力价格飙升。\n【选项】A) 无中生有识别\nThey try to seek help from gene-editing scientists 说农场主动求助科学家，原文第\n五、六段是科学家自己在研究、不是农场求助，属无中生有。\nB) 时态偷换识别\nThey decide to move to cooler mountainous areas 把原文第三段 will then have\nto be shifted 这一未来「将不得不被迁」错读为已经「决定迁移」，时态与主动性都被\n改了。\nC) ✓ 正确\nhad a serious negative impact on cacao farms' yields ↔\nThey have suffered a lot due to a decrease in produce\n「农场产量受严重负面影响」的预判与 They have suffered a lot due to a decrease in\nproduce 在语义上一致；C 把「产量下滑」前移为原因，更贴合题干「What do we\nlearn about the cacao farms」的视角。\nD) 与原文相反识别\nThey have benefited by raising prices of cocoa beans 说农场因涨价获益，但原文是\n「产量下降→巧克力价格飙升」，价格飙升是消极后果而非农场获益；属与原文相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What are scientists trying to do in the University of California's new bio-sciences building?", "options": ["A) Build rows of refrigerated greenhouses for research on cacao seedlings.", "B) Gene-edit cacao seedlings for them to withstand a drier, warmer climate.", "C) Produce chocolates with the latest gene-editing technology.", "D) Transplant the genes of tougher plants to cacao seedlings."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What are scientists trying to do in the University of California's new bio-sciences\nbuilding? 问的是科学家在特定地点的具体行动，可在原文末段直接找到。\n【拆句】题干 What are scientists trying to do in the University of California's new bio-\nsciences building? 是 What + 进行时疑问句：trying to do 表达科学家此刻正努力做某\n事，in 引导的地点状语限定场景。结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】in this unconventional experiment, scientists will make tiny, precise changes to the DNA of\nthe seedlings to make the cocoa crops survive in warmer and drier climates.\n在这个非常规实验中，科学家将对种苗的 DNA 做出细微而精确的改变，以使可可作物能在\n更暖、更干的气候中存活。\n【选项】A) 答非所问识别\nBuild rows of refrigerated greenhouses for research on cacao seedlings 把原文\ntiny green cacao seedlings are lined up in refrigerated greenhouses 中的「冷藏温\n室」错读为科学家在「建造温室」，但温室只是已有的实验环境，不是科学家正在做的\n目的。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmake tiny, precise changes to the DNA of the seedlings ↔ Gene-edit cacao seedlings\nto make the cocoa crops survive in warmer and drier climates ↔\nfor them to withstand a drier, warmer climate\n「基因编辑可可种苗 + 让其耐暖耐旱」的预判与 Gene-edit cacao seedlings for them\nto withstand a drier, warmer climate 在两个语义成分上都重合。\nC) 过度推断识别\nProduce chocolates with the latest gene-editing technology 把目标直接定为「生\n产巧克力」，但原文目的是先改造种苗使可可作物耐气候，距离「生产巧克力」还有多\n步，属过度推断。\nD) 无中生有识别\nTransplant the genes of tougher plants to cacao seedlings 说把更耐性强植物的基\n因移植到可可种苗，原文只说对种苗 DNA 做精细修改，未提「移植其他物种基因」，\n属无中生有。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
